9535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186073886 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490281 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186073887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490263 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186073887 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490283 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186073888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490233 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186073888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490252 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186073888 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490292 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186073889 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490293 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186073889 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490297 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186073975 1050 420.0 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186073981 1050 420.0 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186074967 1064 "Sometimes, we are given a ratio and want to find an amount. In these cases, it's helpful to use equivalent fractions.\n\nFor example, if the ratio of cows to sheep on Margaret's farm is $11:7$, and she has $22$ cows, how many sheep are there on the farm?\n\nTo solve this, we start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}11}:{\\color{red}7}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}11}{\\color{red}7}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of cows and the denominator represents the number of sheep.\n\nSince there are $22$ cows on the farm, we would like a numerator of $22$. We make this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}11}\\times 2}{{\\color{red}7}\\times 2} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}22}}{{\\color{red}14}}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is now the number of sheep on the farm if there are $22$ cows. Therefore, on Margaret's farm, there are $\\color{red}14$ sheep." 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186074967 1064 "Sometimes, we are given a ratio and want to find an amount. In these cases, it's helpful to use equivalent fractions. \n\nFor example, if the ratio of cows to sheep on Margaret's farm is $11:7$, and she has $22$ cows, how many sheep are there on the farm?\n\nTo solve this, we start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}11}:{\\color{red}7}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}11}{\\color{red}7}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of cows and the denominator represents the number of sheep.\n\nSince there are $22$ cows on the farm, we would like a numerator of $22.$ We make this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}11}\\times 2}{{\\color{red}7}\\times 2} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}22}}{{\\color{red}14}}\n$$\n\nThe denominator is now the number of sheep on the farm if there are $22$ cows. Therefore, on Margaret's farm, there are $\\color{red}14$ sheep." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186074970 1064 "A **unit rate** is a comparison of two quantities where one of the terms has a quantity of $1$. This means the ratio, in fraction form, has a denominator of $1$.\n\nFor instance, suppose Michael earned $\\[math] 200$ by selling $4$ identical tires.\n\nThe ratio of dollars to tires is ${\\color{blue}200}:{\\color{red}4}$. Writing the ratio as a fraction, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}200}}{{\\color{red}4}} \\,\n$$\n\nThe unit rate is how many dollars Michael got for $1$ tire.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}200} \\div 4}{{\\color{red}4} \\div 4} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}50}}{{\\color{red}1}}\n$$\n\nThis is the unit rate. In this case, we say that Michael got $\\color{blue}50$ dollars *per* tire." 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186074970 1064 "A **unit rate** is a comparison of two quantities where one of the terms has a quantity of $1.$ This means the ratio, in fraction form, has a denominator of $1.$\n\nFor instance, suppose Michael earned $\\[math] 200$ by selling $4$ identical tires.\n\nThe ratio of dollars to tires is ${\\color{blue}200}:{\\color{red}4}.$ Writing the ratio as a fraction, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}200}}{{\\color{red}4}} \\, .\n$$\n\nThe unit rate is how many dollars Michael got for $1$ tire.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}200} \\div 4}{{\\color{red}4} \\div 4} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}50}}{{\\color{red}1}}\n$$\n\nThis is the unit rate. In this case, we say that Michael got $\\color{blue}50$ dollars *per* tire." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186083179 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490237 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186083179 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490248 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186083179 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490294 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186083180 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490280 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186083180 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490288 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186100818 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490225 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186100818 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490242 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186100818 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490258 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186100818 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490321 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186100885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490267 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186100885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490305 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186100960 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490276 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186105826 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490249 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186117475 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117476 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186117475 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117478 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186117475 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117479 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186117475 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117480 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186117475 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489715 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186117475 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489762 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186117475 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489776 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186117475 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490021 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186117475 1078 #edb/ref 17592186490021 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186119802 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490284 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186119934 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490266 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186120219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186120219 1071 "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(6, 0, 2)$ and $B(1, 1, 3)$, and is parallel to the vector $v = ⟨3, 1,-2⟩$." 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186120219 1071 "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(6,0,2)$ and $B(1,1,3)$, and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨3,1,-2⟩.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186120219 1074 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). We already have one vector $v=⟨3,1,-2⟩.$ Let's also use the displacement vector $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,1,3⟩-⟨6,0,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-5,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $a=⟨6,0,2⟩$ of the point $A,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =a+sv+t\\overset{\\to }{AB} \\\\ r & =⟨6,0,2⟩+s⟨3,1,-2⟩+t⟨-5,1,1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186129263 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490290 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186129309 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490224 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186129309 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490256 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186129310 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490236 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186142780 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490268 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186142796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490269 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186155925 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155926 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186155925 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155927 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186155925 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155928 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186155925 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155929 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186155925 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155930 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186155925 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489749 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186155925 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489772 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186155925 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489774 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186155925 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489946 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186155925 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489969 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186155925 1078 #edb/ref 17592186489969 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186155961 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155963 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186155961 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155964 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186155961 1075 #edb/ref 17592186155965 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186155961 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489630 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186155961 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489765 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186155961 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489873 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186155961 1078 #edb/ref 17592186489630 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186157276 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490324 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186157277 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490244 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186157277 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490247 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186157277 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490328 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186157834 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186157834 1071 "![](images/8b/8b694a0907a5cb1c33d44c921b01b8e351059ce3e695485d79b72051f7ed764a.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $x^{2} + (y - 1)^{2} = 1$ and the line $y = 1$, as shown above. What is\n$∬_(D)y^{2}dA$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186157834 1071 "![](images/8b/8b694a0907a5cb1c33d44c921b01b8e351059ce3e695485d79b72051f7ed764a.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1$ and the line $y=1,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186157834 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/68/68f70968b0dd256159dbfdcbfde4f2f63578237db1770e019c8fb19a5a638eaa.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186157839 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155955 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186157839 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at $(0,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar 