nt }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\frac{1}{3}(0-3\\sqrt{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}[\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186160486 1075 #edb/ref 17592186160487 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186160486 1075 #edb/ref 17592186160488 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186160486 1075 #edb/ref 17592186160489 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186160486 1075 #edb/ref 17592186160490 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186160486 1075 #edb/ref 17592186160491 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186160486 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489566 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186160486 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489601 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186160486 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489709 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186160486 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489976 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186160486 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489987 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186160486 1078 #edb/ref 17592186489709 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186161771 1071 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n![](images/49/494cd45f9c85aaa8703ed4c5a0cac805b69cd91bbef979a2c3ff1ffac1592763.png)" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186161771 1071 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n![](images/fb/fb428c39bbc03be798bca2463658676de5c684396001cf547e9395352f456bc9.svg)" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186161771 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P=P^T.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q=Q^T.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since $r_{12} \\neq r_{21},$ we obtain $R \\neq R^T.$\n![](images/3f/3f91ec40e717eac93bb38f9c13f7b1700e84ac109ccceaffbb8473ba8d6b4e34.svg)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\"" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186161771 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P=P^T.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q=Q^T.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since $r_{12} \\neq r_{21},$ we obtain $R \\neq R^T.$\n![](images/4d/4db4bdfa8aaeac26238404107f195847eaccde9e595a7d1a0678053e2b149d0e.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\"" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186161816 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+A^T$ is always symmetric.\n\n- The difference between two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\n- The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric." 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186161816 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+A^T$ is always symmetric.\n\nII. The difference between two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\nIII. The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186161816 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the sum or difference between two symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n![](images/81/812e9888b920e0338fd72116464b8b96c8c272f95f180cd46aa3f1db9afac8d2.svg)\nTheir product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186161816 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the sum or difference between two symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n![](images/ca/ca86224294fba346835dab8fcfd7a045dfe1bda723499dceca4b60339ece6300.png)\nTheir product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186161979 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490264 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186161979 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490309 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186161979 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490315 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186162024 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490230 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186162156 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490216 1