 main diagonal. \n\nFor example, the matrix \n\n![](images/0f/0f62a89f8da1ab7bfb86d46fddb441e95e5e3a9554e5a88662691b4df40e19af.svg)\n\nis symmetric since $A^T = A.$ \n\nOn the other hand, the matrix \n\n![](images/78/780de3c3f66b1037e4152224f4aa98b65b8b1b859adfe7c3881bb830d7eecd66.svg)\n\nis not symmetric. Indeed, since $b_{12} \\neq b_{21},$ we have $B^T \\neq B.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186163222 1064 "A square matrix $A$ is **symmetric** if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$A^T = A$$\n\nEquivalently, an $n\\times n$ matrix is symmetric if $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $1 \\leq i,j\\leq n.$\n\nIf $A$ is symmetric, this really means that it is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n$$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nis symmetric since $A^T = A.$\n\nOn the other hand, the matrix\n\n$$B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nis not symmetric. Indeed, since $b_{12} \\neq b_{21},$ we have $B^T \\neq B.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186163254 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is a symmetric matrix. Then, we have the following properties:\n\n- $-A$ is symmetric\n\n- $A^m$ is symmetric for any positive integer $m$\n\n- $A^{-1}$ is symmetric for nonsingular $A$\n\nIf $A$ and $B$ are $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices, we have the following properties:\n\n- $A+B$ is symmetric\n\n- The product $A\\cdot B$ is *not* always symmetric. For example, if \n![](images/e4/e41d28b6c6562ada530024fe6ab29cc16447dce14b2f741b8b35758618c78103.svg)\nthen the product $A\\cdot B$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is *not* symmetric.\n\nFinally, the following two properties can be used to construct symmetric matrices:\n\n- $A + A^T$ is symmetric for any *square* matrix $A$\n\n- $A^T A$ and $A A^T$ are symmetric for *any* matrix $A$\n\nThe last property is true for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix. This property is particularly useful, as we'll discover in future lessons." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186163254 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is a symmetric matrix. Then, we have the following properties:\n\n- $-A$ is symmetric\n- $A^m$ is symmetric for any positive integer $m$\n- $A^{-1}$ is symmetric for nonsingular $A$\n\nIf $A$ and $B$ are $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices, we have the following properties:\n\n- $A+B$ is symmetric\n- The product $A\\cdot B$ is *not* always symmetric. For example, if $$A = [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}], \\qquad B = [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$$ then the product $A\\cdot B$ is $$\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}$$ which is *not* symmetric.\n\nFinally, the following two properties can be used to construct symmetric matrices:\n\n- $A + A^T$ is symmetric for any *square* matrix $A$\n- $A^T A$ and $A A^T$ are symmetric for *any* matrix $A$\n\nThe last property is true for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix. This property is particularly useful, as we'll discover in future lessons." 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186166665 1074 "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/59/5973b712a92cadd7bc301f7e8e859a1a53a551cfeee0b0ac1404ddbfc352ac56.png)\n\nHere, $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n![](images/a7/a7c5af2adc67390439a5d46933083b796c30fc55f0f1f5ff8cd9d374a905695d.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186166665 1074 "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/60/6044f7e9a7a3e784da6affc47c4cfcedce461feb44057992f39fd3154e5cb846.svg)\n\nHere, $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n![](images/d4/d4788968ba8347e6288028e679a0df0c3629c292aaca4552293fc6f9380486ef.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166666 1074 "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/e3/e31c12bf515605c7cb46d96a55a8e2a3cab7eaeb5897bb04cfbe87d4d6cc6875.png)\n\nHere, $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 37\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n![](images/5e/5e7f6f2abab85141723e1ef5bd97157968584623433cd5a3e4d0454a39ef000b.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4}{7})}^{0}(\\frac{3}{7})+{(\\frac{4}{7})}^{1}(\\frac{3}{7})+{(\\frac{4}{7})}^{2}(\\frac{3}{7})+{(\\frac{4}{7})}^{3}(\\frac{3}{7}) \\\\  & ≈0.893\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto  $3$ decimal places." 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186166666 1074 "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/f6/f6da43d5d0276991429f04048f3ea4e7ec2b4d9727abc71f4d7879cefd71797b.svg)\n\nHere, $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 37\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n![](images/bc/bcba51c019bfe72f14e0fb7d6465abf3595b03c8e70598231934f5166393873b.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4}{7})}^{0}(\\frac{3}{7})+{(\\frac{4}{7})}^{1}(\\frac{3}{7})+{(\\frac{4}{7})}^{2}(\\frac{3}{7})+{(\\frac{4}{7})}^{3}(\\frac{3}{7}) \\\\  & ≈0.893\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto  $3$ decimal places." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166667 1074 "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/8f/8fe2487af4333bae81059a2ab221b3988bbdf27f4f51521683addd789c31a4b9.svg)\n\nHere, $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 15\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n![](images/a4/a44212ab1418417646876630a4cea241bfefaf063b26251dc5f157824cc4805d.svg)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\le X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{2,3\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{1}(\\frac{1}{5})+{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166667 1074 "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/e3/e31c12bf515605c7cb46d96a55a8e2a3cab7eaeb5897bb04cfbe87d4d6cc6875.png)\n\nHere, $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 15\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n![](images/cb/cb4d54529b778e27c44ea6611a366eef6e540d9de5343e3425797f03d51c54de.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\le X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {2,3}) \\\\  & =P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{1}(\\frac{1}{5})+{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186166668 1074 "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/0d/0d3d01e8f77c51e75d7dfce4ef2b60e8a61be89eaf752b2203857d97e77c6127.svg)\n\nHere, $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 56\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n![](images/db/db0084ae705309592c8d309304e26a46ecde6e6a1b85ef07b57b6f1a394f26fa.svg)\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X > 2.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =1-P(X\\le 2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{0}(\\frac{5}{6})+{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{1}(\\frac{5}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =1-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{35}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166668 1074 "If $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/ab/ab3f034397a81b2369008754aef3c05f998f180b5127e543a05ab408c743cf8a.png)\n\nHere, $X \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac 56\\right),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n![](images/ef/efc7a45d9b19867340454ad32abe129214dcca021394f259f79d1e26643da5c7.png)\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X > 2.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =1-P(X\\le 2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {1,2}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{0}(\\frac{5}{6})+{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{1}(\\frac{5}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =1-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{35}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186166827 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490238 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166827 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490272 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166827 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490310 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166827 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490311 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166827 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490323 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490218 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490243 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490245 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490275 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490302 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490229 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490255 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490274 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490317 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490319 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490214 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490215 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490240 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490300 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186166830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490325 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186167045 1050 1140.0 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186168075 1064 "Suppose that we have a sequence of Bernoulli trials, each resulting in success or failure, and with a probability of success $p.$ Let the random variable $X$ represent the number of Bernoulli trials *until the first success*.\n\nIt can be shown that $X$ follows a **geometric distribution** and has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/13/13d0eefea25458f7778607118a7673506900d72ee5b8a41826378c8737317e9e.svg)\n\nLet's break this formula down a bit:\n\n- The factor $(1-p)^{x-1}$ represents the probability of getting exactly $(x-1)$ failures, each with probability $(1-p).$\n\n- The factor ${\\color{purple}{p}}$ represents the probability of getting a single success.\n\n- The condition $x=1,2,3,\\ldots$ indicates that the sequence of Bernoulli trials terminates after a minimum of $1$ trial, and there is no maximum number of trials.\n\nIf a random variable $X$ follows a geometric distribution, we say it is a geometric random variable and  write \n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p).\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186168075 1064 "Suppose that we have a sequence of Bernoulli trials, each resulting in success or failure, and with a probability of success $p.$ Let the random variable $X$ represent the number of Bernoulli trials *until the first success*.\n\nIt can be shown that $X$ follows a **geometric distribution** and has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p,\\, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's break this formula down a bit:\n\n- The factor $(1-p)^{x-1}$ represents the probability of getting exactly $(x-1)$ failures, each with probability $(1-p).$\n\n- The factor ${\\color{purple}{p}}$ represents the probability of getting a single success.\n\n- The condition $x=1,2,3,\\ldots$ indicates that the sequence of Bernoulli trials terminates after a minimum of $1$ trial, and there is no maximum number of trials.\n\nIf a random variable $X$ follows a geometric distribution, we say it is a geometric random variable and  write \n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p).\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186168108 1064 "You might be wondering where the geometric distribution gets its name. The answer is that the probability mass function forms a geometric sequence! \n\nSuppose that $X\\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p).$ Then, the probability mass function for this geometric distribution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-p)^{x-1}p \\\\ f(x) & =p(1-p)^{x-1} \\\\ {{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{x}} & ={{p}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}\\cdot ({{1-p}_{⏟}}_{r})^{x-1} \\\\ {a}_{x} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we see that the probability mass function can be interpreted as a geometric sequence where the first term is $a_1=p,$ and the common ratio is $r=1-p.$\n\nMoreover, notice that $f(x)$ satisfies all the criteria to be a probability mass function, namely\n\n- $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1$\n\nCriterion 1 is clearly true because $0 \\leq p \\leq 1.$ \n\nTo show that criterion 2 is true, we simply need to calculate the sum of this infinite geometric series, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{x\\in S}f(x) & ={\\sum }_{x=1}^{\\infty }p(1-p)^{x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{p}{1-(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\frac{p}{p} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is indeed a probability distribution." 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186168108 1064 "You might be wondering where the geometric distribution gets its name. The answer is that the probability mass function forms a geometric sequence! \n\nSuppose that $X\\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p).$ Then, the probability mass function for this geometric distribution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-p)^{x-1}p \\\\ f(x) & =p(1-p)^{x-1} \\\\ {{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{x}} & ={{p}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}\\cdot ({{1-p}_{⏟}}_{r})^{x-1} \\\\ {a}_{x} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we see that the probability mass function can be interpreted as a geometric sequence where the first term is $a_1=p,$ and the common ratio is $r=1-p.$\n\nMoreover, notice that $f(x)$ satisfies all the criteria to be a probability mass function, namely\n\n1\\. $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n2\\. $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1$\n\nCriterion 1 is clearly true because $0 \\leq p \\leq 1.$ \n\nTo show that criterion 2 is true, we simply need to calculate the sum of this infinite geometric series, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{x\\in S}f(x) & ={\\sum }_{x=1}^{\\infty }p(1-p)^{x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{p}{1-(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\frac{p}{p} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is indeed a probability distribution." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186177867 1075 #edb/ref 17592186177868 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186177867 1075 #edb/ref 17592186177869 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186177867 1075 #edb/ref 17592186177870 13194139535279 17592