 17592186489954 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186178221 1078 #edb/ref 17592186489579 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186178227 1078 #edb/ref 17592186178230 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186179404 1075 #edb/ref 17592186179405 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186179404 1075 #edb/ref 17592186179406 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186179404 1075 #edb/ref 17592186179407 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186179404 1075 #edb/ref 17592186179408 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186179404 1075 #edb/ref 17592186179409 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186179404 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489587 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186179404 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489614 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186179404 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489650 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186179404 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489781 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186179404 1075 #edb/ref 17592186489967 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186179404 1078 #edb/ref 17592186489650 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186179427 1071 "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\n- $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = x^4y\\,\\mathbf{i} - 2x^3y^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$\n\n- $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\sin x \\sin y\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\cos x \\cos y\\,\\mathbf{j}$\n\n- $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = 4x^2y\\,\\mathbf{i} -4x^2y^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186179427 1071 "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = x^4y\\,\\mathbf{i} - 2x^3y^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\sin x \\sin y\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\cos x \\cos y\\,\\mathbf{j}$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = 4x^2y\\,\\mathbf{i} -4x^2y^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186179651 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490222 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186179651 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490228 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186179651 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490231 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186179652 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490278 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186179652 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490322 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186179688 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490261 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186179688 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490329 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186179690 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490220 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186179690 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490254 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186179765 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490304 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186179766 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490314 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186179766 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490318 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186179774 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490212 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186179855 1050 600.0 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186179868 1050 780.0 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186180033 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186180033 1071 "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$F(x, y) = x^{2}i - 2xyj$?" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186180033 1071 "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-2xy\\,j?$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186180033 1074 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-2xy\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=-2xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x+(-2x)=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}={x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=2xy.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={x}^{2}y.\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186180085 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186180085 1071 "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$F(x, y) = (1 - 2xy)i - y^{2}j$?" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186180085 1071 "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$F(x,y)=(1-2xy)\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j?$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186180085 1074 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)=(1-2xy)\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)=1-2xy$ and $Q(x,y)=-{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a no stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=-2y+(-2y)=-4y\\ne 0.\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186180091 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178215 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186180092 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186180092 1071 "Construct the vector field that has the stream function $ψ(x, y) = y^{2}e^{xy} + x^{2}$." 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186180092 1071 "Construct the vector field that has the stream function $ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186180092 1074 "A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $ψ(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}{e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y{e}^{xy}+{y}^{2}x{e}^{xy} \\\\  & =y{e}^{xy}(2+yx), \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}{e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-({y}^{3}{e}^{xy}+2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}+{x}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & =P\\,i+Q\\,j \\\\  & =y{e}^{xy}(2+yx)\\,i-({y}^{3}{e}^{xy}+2x)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186180093 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186180093 1071 "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\n1. $F(x, y) = 4xyi + y^{4}j$\n2. $F(x, y) = \\cos (3x)i + 3y\\sin (3x)j$\n3. $F(x, y) = e^{xy}i - ye^{x}j$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186180093 1071 "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nI. $F(x,y)=4xy\\,i+{y}^{4}\\,j$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,i+3y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,j$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)={e}^{xy}\\,i-y{e}^{x}\\,j$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186180093 1074 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field I doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =4{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=4y+4{y}^{3}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=y{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=y{e}^{xy}+(-{e}^{x})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186180185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178654 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186180310 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186180310 1071 "Given that $F(x, y) = ⟨x, x^{2}y⟩$ and $f(x, y) = xy$, calculate $∇ \\cdot (fF)$." 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186180310 1071 "Given that $F(x,y)=⟨x,{x}^{2}y⟩$ and $f(x,y)=xy,$ calculate $∇\\cdot (f\\,F).$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186180310 1074 "We will use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot (f\\,F)=f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇\\cdot F$ and $∇f.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨y,x⟩, \\\\ ∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y)=1+{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (f\\,F) & =f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f \\\\  & =xy(1+{x}^{2})+⟨x,{x}^{2}y⟩\\cdot ⟨y,x⟩ \\\\  & =xy+{x}^{3}y+xy+{x}^{3}y \\\\  & =2xy+2{x}^{3}y \\\\  & =2xy(1+{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186180709 1064 "Recall that for functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ the product rule for differentiation is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(f\\cdot g) = f\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(g) +  g\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(f).\n$$\n\nThere is an analogous rule for computing the divergence of the product of a vector field and a scalar field.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field and $f$ is a scalar field. Then, we have the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\nabla \\cdot (f\\,\\mathbf F) = f\\, \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf {F}+  \\mathbf F \\cdot \\nabla \\! f\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $\\nabla f$ is the gradient of $f.$\n\nNotice that the del operator $\\nabla$ acts on the terms in the same way as the $\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}$ operator in the product rule for differentiation!\n\nThere are a few other tips that we can use to remember this rule:\n\n- The term $\\nabla \\cdot (f\\,\\mathbf F)$ on the left-hand side must be a scalar field because it's a divergence. So, the two terms on the right-hand side must be scalar fields, too.\n\n- The term $f\\, \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf {F}$ on the right-hand side is a scalar field because both $f$ and $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F$ are scalar fields, and the product of two scalar fields is a scalar field. Note that this \"product\" is just regular multiplication.\n\n- The term $\\mathbf F \\cdot \\nabla \\! f$ on the right-hand side is a scalar field because both $\\mathbf F$ and $\\nabla f$ are *vector* fields, and the *dot product* of two vector fields is a scalar field." 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186180709 1064 "Recall that for functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ the product rule for differentiation is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(f\\cdot g) = f\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(g) +  g\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(f).\n$$\n\nThere is an analogous rule for computing the divergence of the product of a vector field and a scalar field.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field and $f$ is a scalar field. Then, we have the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\nabla \\cdot (f\\,\\mathbf F) = f\\, \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf {F}+  \\mathbf F \\cdot \\nabla \\! f\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\nabla f$ is the gradient of $f.$\n\nNotice that the del operator $\\nabla$ acts on the terms in the same way as the $\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}$ operator in the product rule for differentiation!\n\nThere are a few other tips that we can use to remember this rule:\n\n- The term $\\nabla \\cdot (f\\,\\mathbf F)$ on the left-hand side must be a scalar field because it's a divergence. So, the two terms on the right-hand side must be scalar fields, too.\n\n- The term $f\\, \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf {F}$ on the right-hand side is a scalar field because both $f$ and $\\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F$ are scalar fields, and the product of two scalar fields is a scalar field. Note that this \"product\" is just regular multiplication.\n\n- The term $\\mathbf F \\cdot \\nabla \\! f$ on the right-hand side is a scalar field because both $\\mathbf F$ and $\\nabla f$ are *vector* fields, and the *dot product* of two vector fields is a scalar field." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186180726 1064 "Let $\\psi(x,y)$ be a two-variable function defined on a simply connected open set $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2.$ We know that for a point $(x_0,y_0)\\in D,$ the vector\n\n$$\n\\left.\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf i -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf j\\right)\\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}\n$$\n\nis *tangential* to the level curve $\\psi(x,y) = \\psi(x_0,y_0).$ \n\nBy computing this tangent vector at *every* point, we can generate a vector *field* tangential to the level curves of $\\psi$ at every point.\n\nWith that in mind, let \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)= P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\n \n\nbe a vector field defined on $D.$ A function $\\psi(x,y)$ is said to be a **stream function** of $\\mathbf F$ if it satisfies\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad \nQ = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's consider the function \n\n$$\n\\psi(x,y) = x^2+y^2.\n$$\n\nTo construct a vector field with this stream function, we first compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the vector field with the stream function $\\psi$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & =P\\,i+Q\\,j=2y\\,i-2x\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nA diagram showing the surface $z = \\psi(x,y),$ its level curves, and the vector field $\\mathbf F$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/69/696e6c623dbc3ecfae2643a7317d3d176294624289ecf6efb16debdc947d0394.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The level curves of $\\psi(x,y)$ are circles of the form $x^2+y^2=c$, and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ in the $xy$-plane is tangential to these level curves.\n\n- Suppose $\\mathbf F$ represents the velocity of a fluid in the $xy$-plane. Then, the level curves of $\\psi(x,y)$ represent the path of a massless particle in the $xy$-plane moving with the flow. For this reason, the level curves of $\\psi$ are called **streamlines.**\n\n- The stream function of a vector field is typically not unique since we can add any constant to $\\psi$ to generate another valid stream function." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186180726 1064 "Let $\\psi(x,y)$ be a two-variable function defined on a simply connected open set $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2.$ We know that for a point $(x_0,y_0)\\in D,$ the vector\n\n$$\\left.\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf i -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf j\\right)\\right|_{(x_0,y_0)}$$ is *tangential* to the level curve $\\psi(x,y) = \\psi(x_0,y_0).$\n\nBy computing this tangent vector at *every* point, we can generate a vector *field* tangential to the level curves of $\\psi$ at every point.\n\nWith that in mind, let\n\n$$\\mathbf F(x,y)= P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{j}$$\n\nbe a vector field defined on $D.$ A function $\\psi(x,y)$ is said to be a **stream function** of $\\mathbf F$ if it satisfies\n\n$$P = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad Q = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}.$$\n\nFor example, let's consider the function\n\n$$\\psi(x,y) = x^2+y^2.$$\n\nTo construct a vector field with this stream function, we first compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\ & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\ & =2y, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\ & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) \\\\ & =-2x.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThus, the vector field with the stream function $\\psi$ is given by\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & =P\\,i+Q\\,j=2y\\,i-2x\\,j.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nA diagram showing the surface $z = \\psi(x,y),$ its level curves, and the vector field $\\mathbf F$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/69/696e6c623dbc3ecfae2643a7317d3d176294624289ecf6efb16debdc947d0394.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The level curves of $\\psi(x,y)$ are circles of the form $x^2+y^2=c$, and the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ in the $xy$-plane is tangential to these level curves.\n- Suppose $\\mathbf F$ represents the velocity of a fluid in the $xy$-plane. Then, the level curves of $\\psi(x,y)$ represent the path of a massless particle in the $xy$-plane moving with the flow. For this reason, the level curves of $\\psi$ are called **streamlines.**\n- The stream function of a vector field is typically not unique since we can add any constant to $\\psi$ to generate another valid stream function." 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186180759 1064 "Let's find a stream function for the following vector field:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = e^x \\sin y\\,\\mathbf{i} + e^x \\cos y\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nNote that our vector field $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ has the simply connected domain $D=\\mathbb{R}^2,$ where \n\n$$\nP(x,y) = e^x\\sin y, \\qquad\nQ(x,y) = e^x\\cos y.\n$$\n\nLet's start by checking that a stream function exists. Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\mathbf F$ has a stream function since $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0.$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $\\psi(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $\\mathbf F= P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ if \n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad Q = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y} = e^x\\sin y, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x} = -e^x\\cos y.\n$$\n\nTo find a stream function, we **partially integrate** these two equations and compare the results:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y} = e^x\\sin y\n$$\n\nPartially integrating this equation with respect to $y$ (that is, treating $x$ as though it were a constant), we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y+f(x) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+f(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is an arbitrary function.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x} = -e^x\\cos y.\n$$\n\nPartially integrating this equation with respect to $x$ (that is, treating $y$ as though it were a constant), we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =\\int -{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+g(y) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is an arbitrary function.\n\nComparing our two expressions for $\\psi,$ we get equality by setting $f=g=0.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\psi(x,y) = -e^x \\cos y.\n$$\n\nFinally, remember that the streamlines of $\\mathbf F$ are given by the level curves of $\\psi.$ So, to find the streamlines of $\\mathbf F,$ we plot the curves $\\psi = k$ for different values of $k.$ The level curves of $\\psi$ are always tangential to $\\mathbf F.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186180759 1064 "Let's find a stream function for the following vector field:\n\n$$\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = e^x \\sin y\\,\\mathbf{i} + e^x \\cos y\\,\\mathbf{j}$$\n\nNote that our vector field $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ has the simply connected domain $D=\\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n\n$$P(x,y) = e^x\\sin y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = e^x\\cos y.$$\n\nLet's start by checking that a stream function exists. Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}y.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nHence, $\\mathbf F$ has a stream function since $\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0.$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $\\psi(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $\\mathbf F= P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ if\n\n$$P = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad Q = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}.$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y} = e^x\\sin y, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x} = -e^x\\cos y.$$\n\nTo find a stream function, we **partially integrate** these two equations and compare the results:\n\n- For the first equation, we have $$\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y} = e^x\\sin y$$ Partially integrating this equation with respect to $y$ (that is, treating $x$ as though it were a constant), we get $$\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =\\int {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}y\\,\\text{d}y+f(x) \\\\ & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}y+f(x),\\end{aligned}$$ where $f$ is an arbitrary function.\n- For the second equation, we have $$\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x} = -e^x\\cos y.$$ Partially integrating this equation with respect to $x$ (that is, treating $y$ as though it were a constant), we get $$\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =\\int -{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}y\\,\\text{d}x+g(y) \\\\ & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}y+g(y)\\end{aligned}$$ where $g$ is an arbitrary function.\n\nComparing our two expressions for $\\psi,$ we get equality by setting $f=g=0.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\\psi(x,y) = -e^x \\cos y.$$\n\nFinally, remember that the streamlines of $\\mathbf F$ are given by the level curves of $\\psi.$ So, to find the streamlines of $\\mathbf F,$ we plot the curves $\\psi = k$ for different values of $k.$ The level curves of $\\psi$ are always tangential to $\\mathbf F.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186180763 1064 "Not all vector fields have an associated stream function! So, it's useful to have some way of determining whether a given vector field has a stream function or not. For this, we have the following theorem:\n\n*Let $\\mathbf F= P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ be a vector field defined in a simply connected open set $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2.$ The vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ if and only if*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0.\n$$\n\nIn other words, the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ must be zero everywhere for a stream function to exist. \n\nWe won't give a detailed proof of this result here, but it's quite easy to see why it may be true. \n\nRecall that if $\\psi(x,y)$ is a stream function of $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ if it satisfies\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad \nQ = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the divergence of this vector field, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂x∂y}-\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂y∂x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂x∂y}-\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂x∂y} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186180763 1064 "Not all vector fields have an associated stream function! So, it's useful to have some way of determining whether a given vector field has a stream function or not. For this, we have the following theorem:\n\n*Let $\\mathbf F= P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ be a vector field defined in a simply connected open set $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2.$ The vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ if and only if* $$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0.$$\n\nIn other words, the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ must be zero everywhere for a stream function to exist.\n\nWe won't give a detailed proof of this result here, but it's quite easy to see why it may be true.\n\nRecall that if $\\psi(x,y)$ is a stream function of $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ if it satisfies\n\n$$P = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad Q = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}.$$\n\nCalculating the divergence of this vector field, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}) \\\\ & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂x∂y}-\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂y∂x} \\\\ & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂x∂y}-\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂x∂y} \\\\ & =0\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186183133 1078 #edb/ref 17592186183136 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186183145 1078 #edb/ref 17592186183147 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186183163 1078 #edb/ref 17592186183168 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186183169 1078 #edb/ref 17592186183173 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186184071 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and  ![](images/a7/a7171d7bdd2b5c79b55ce6bd204e36304640468bd2ad533ac4032cd5a4ddc2ae.svg) \n\nI. $\\textrm{rank}(A)=1$\n\nII. $A$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $2$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186184071 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, and  ![](images/ba/ba5c9db77780e1298423e58aaf6f9a54968db34643cfe98bfce96231ab6d3afc.png) \n\n- $\\textrm{rank}(A)=1$\n\n- $A$ is invertible\n\n- $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $2$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186184071 1074 "Since $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n![](images/ab/ab1cd4be22e0d3344318108914d506f9fc259446654eefff94a36d38afac3f8b.svg)\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that \n![](images/e7/e7660dfd412f13c5d20dd9e77ec439cdbf2916ebfbccc383d13b5f9ed4032cda.svg) \nwhich means that $\\textrm{nullity}(A) =2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n = 3,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\textrm{nullity}(A) = 2 \\neq 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ cannot be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 1,$ we have that $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $1$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186184071 1074 "Since $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n![](images/db/db1215d898d9a9af40907393d15bf2a03d1f709d857925e9756cfe15db2f9a07.png)\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that \n![](images/59/5998f460a1889d2d938c3d530ef86c37f4a76f746ce0885e7892407aa7a2b02f.png) \nwhich means that $\\textrm{nullity}(A) =2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n = 3,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =3 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\textrm{nullity}(A) = 2 \\neq 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ cannot be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(A) = 1,$ we have that $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is a $1$-dimensional subspace of $\\Bbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186184172 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490217 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186184172 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490316 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186184219 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490326 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186184219 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490327 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186184421 1050 420.0 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186184754 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186184754 1071 "Consider a $5 \\cdot 8$ matrix $A$. If $dim(Null(A)) = 3$, then $rank(A) =$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186184754 1071 "Consider a $5\\times 8$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim(Null(}A))=3,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186184754 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =8 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =8-3 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186184756 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186184756 1071 "Consider a $6 \\cdot 7$ matrix $A$. If $rank(A) = 5$ then $nullity(A) =$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186184756 1071 "Consider a $6\\times 7$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{rank}(A)=5$ then $\\text{nullity}(A)=$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186184756 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 5+\\text{nullity}(A) & =7 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =7-5 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186184759 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186184759 1071 "Find $\\text{rank}(A)$ given that $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186184759 1071 "Find $rank(A)$ given that $Null(A) = {\\begin{bmatrix}2x_{2} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{2} + x_{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\mid x_{2}, x_{3} ∈ R}$." 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186184759 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\text{nullity}(A)=2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186184760 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186184760 1071 "Let $A$ be a $3 \\cdot 6$ matrix. If $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}}$ is a basis of $Col(A)$, find $dim(Null(A))$." 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186184760 1071 "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 6$ matrix. If $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186184760 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=6,$ and since $B$ is formed by $3$ vectors, $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =6 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =6-3 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186185320 1064 "When we reduce a matrix to row echelon form, the number of *pivot* columns plus the number of *non-pivot* columns always equals the total number of columns of the matrix. \n\nBy translating this into the language of vector spaces, we get the **rank-nullity theorem**. The rank-nullity theorem relates the rank of a matrix to the dimension of its null space:\n\n*Let $A$ be a matrix (not necessarily square) with $n$ columns. Then,*\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Col}(A)) + \\textrm{dim(Null}(A)) = \\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n),\n$$\n\n*or, equivalently,*\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that for some matrix $A,$ we have\n\n![](images/30/30f6472f02a4ac47f285fff05e0cdbf56367acdd18231271dc3dccf0040901bd.svg)\n\nWe can use the rank-nullity theorem to calculate $\\textrm{rank}(A).$\n\nIn this case, we must have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, we must have $\\textrm{nullity}(A)=2,$ since \n![](images/b5/b5f38590a71a2f8e1f29a5ba98920a10037737c1ebcbd3fd60f08a3e47555141.svg)\n\nTherefore, using the rank-nullity theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186185320 1064 "When we reduce a matrix to row echelon form, the number of *pivot* columns plus the number of *non-pivot* columns always equals the total number of columns of the matrix. \n\nBy translating this into the language of vector spaces, we get the **rank-nullity theorem**. The rank-nullity theorem relates the rank of a matrix to the dimension of its null space:\n\n> *Let $A$ be a matrix (not necessarily square) with $n$ columns. Then,*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\textrm{dim(Col}(A)) + \\textrm{dim(Null}(A)) = \\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n),\n> $$\n> \n> \n> *or, equivalently,*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n> $$\n\nFor example, suppose that for some matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can use the rank-nullity theorem to calculate $\\textrm{rank}(A).$\n\nIn this case, we must have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, we must have $\\textrm{nullity}(A)=2,$ since \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the rank-nullity theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186198431 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490298 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186244108 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -8+k \\\\ 3 & 5k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =2k[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}k-10 \\\\ -6+5k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4k \\\\ 2k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}k-10=-4k, \\\\ -6+5k=2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nk-10=-4k\\,⟹\\,k=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=2$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6+5k & =2k \\\\ -6+5(2) & =2(2) \\\\ 4 & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=2.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186244108 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -8+k \\\\ 3 & 5k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =2k[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}k-10 \\\\ -6+5k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4k \\\\ 2k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}k-10=-4k, \\\\ -6+5k=2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nk-10=-4k\\,⟹\\,k=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=2$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6+5k & =2k \\\\ -6+5(2) & =2(2) \\\\ 4 & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=2.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186272246 1071 "Let $A$ be a $5\\times 7$ matrix. If $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},{b}_{4}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186272246 1071 "Let $A$ be a $5\\times 7$ matrix. If $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},{b}_{4}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186321066 1071 "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 5$ matrix. If $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186321066 1071 "Let $A$ be a $3\\times 5$ matrix. If $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 false] [17592186351219 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $15{x}_{1},$ where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045