5 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490210 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048924 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490210 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049143 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490210 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049253 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490210 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049640 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490210 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049752 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490210 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050462 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490210 1089 #edb/ref 17592186490211 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490210 1090 #uuid "d35884e6-1669-5364-a41a-a52efb1220e4" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490211 1084 #edb/ref 17592186490335 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490211 1084 #edb/ref 17592186490337 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490211 1084 #edb/ref 17592186490345 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490211 1084 #edb/ref 17592186490349 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490211 1084 #edb/ref 17592186490357 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490211 1084 #edb/ref 17592186490384 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490211 1084 #edb/ref 17592186490387 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490211 1085 "A store in a small town sells candy." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490211 1086 #uuid "3ef56e99-ee02-5a7a-b434-af1fab1ad5e2" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490211 1087 #edb/ref 17592186490345 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490174 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490212 1069 "q-162160" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490212 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490212 1071 "Which of the following represents the streamlines of the vector field $F(x,y)=(\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}})i+(\\frac{2x}{y-{x}^{2}})j$ over the domain $D=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>{x}^{2}\\}.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490212 1072 #uuid "03021383-b99c-5e38-823a-268b5d1215a6" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490212 1074 "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=(\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}})i+(\\frac{2x}{y-{x}^{2}})j\n$$\n\n is a vector field with simply connected domain $D,$ where $P(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{y-{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{(y-{x}^{2})^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-\\frac{2x}{(y-{x}^{2})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{2x}{(y-{x}^{2})^{2}}+(-\\frac{2x}{(y-{x}^{2})^{2}})=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=-\\frac{2x}{y-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-{x}^{2})+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-{x}^{2})+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-{x}^{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curves of the stream function $ψ(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-{x}^{2})$ given by the equations $ψ(x,y)=c.$\n\nNotice that the range of $ψ(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-{x}^{2}) & =c \\\\ y-{x}^{2} & ={e}^{c} \\\\ y & ={x}^{2}+{e}^{c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of parabolas with the vertex at $(0,{e}^{c}).$ Therefore, the correct graph (along with the vector field $F$) is as follows:\n\n![](images/ed/edc12146ffad4dc46ed6d57b3dfdc9a08ca92fb601ef5e08ed9ccef3a1c1da4c.png)" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490114 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490213 1069 "q-71678" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490213 1071 "Which of the following ordered pairs is a solution to $y=2x-3?$\n\nI. $(-2,-7)$\n\nII. $(0,3)$\n\nIII. $(2,4)$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490213 1072 #uuid "04603f4d-d37d-5464-90af-2ef30e760b2c" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490213 1074 "To check whether each point is a solution to the equation, we substitute it into the equation and see if it results in a true statement.\n\nFirst, we substitute $(-2,-7)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\ (-7) & =2(-2)-3 \\\\ -7 & =-4-3 \\\\ -7 & =-7\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a true statement, so $(-2,-7)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\nNext, we substitute $(0,3)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\ (3) & =2(0)-3 \\\\ 3 & =-3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a false statement, so $(0,3)$ is *not* a solution to the equation.\n\nFinally, we substitute $(2,4)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x-3 \\\\ (4) & =2(2)-3 \\\\ 4 & =4-3 \\\\ 4 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is again a false statement, so $(2,4)$ is also *not* a solution to the equation.\n\nSo, out of the three ordered pairs given, the only pair that is a solution to the equation is I $(-2,-7).$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490158 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490214 1069 "q-335508" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490214 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{7}{8}),$ compute $P(X\\ge 3)$ round to $4$ decimal places.\n\n$P(X\\ge 3)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490214 1072 #uuid "04c3981e-56b1-51ed-b49e-4d2d764aacc9" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490214 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{7}{8}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x-1}(\\frac{7}{8}), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X\\ge 3.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 3)=1-P(X<3)\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{0}(\\frac{7}{8})+{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{1}(\\frac{7}{8}) \\\\  & =0.984375.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & =1-0.984375 \\\\  & ≈0.0156.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490125 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490215 1069 "q-130234" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490215 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490215 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{5}{7}),$ compute $P(X\\ge 3)$ rounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490215 1072 #uuid "075d366c-d06a-5f72-a1db-49d33772eb88" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490215 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{5}{7}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x-1}(\\frac{5}{7}), & x=1,2,3… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X\\ge 3.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{7})}^{0}(\\frac{5}{7})+{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{1}(\\frac{5}{7}) \\\\  & ≈0.9184.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & ≈1-0.9184 \\\\  & =0.0816.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490133 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490216 1069 "q-253542" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490216 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490216 1071 "What is the infimum of the set $C=\\{-1,\\,-3,\\,0,\\,2,\\,1,\\,-2\\}?$\n\n$inf(C)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490216 1072 #uuid "0ecf28e6-85ea-58f8-8358-bdfe911fb50e" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490216 1074 "The infimum of a set $C$ is the greatest lower bound of $C.$\n\nIf $C$ is a finite set, the infimum of $C$ equals its minimum. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}inf(C) & =min\\{-1,\\,-3,\\,0,\\,2,\\,1,\\,-2\\}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490217 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490134 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490217 1069 "q-142538" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490217 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490217 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix with $2$ pivot columns?\n\nI. $\\text{rank}(A)=4$\n\nII. $A$ is invertible\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(A)=3$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490217 1072 #uuid "128f0273-6319-516f-a4e8-3d58e084f0a9" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490217 1074 "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false.  Since $A$ has $2$ pivot columns, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For the matrix $A,$ the number of the columns is $n=5.$ Now, since $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, we have that $A$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. According to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 2+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" III only.\"" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490218 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490076 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490218 1069 "q-329137" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490218 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490218 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.75),$ compute $P(X<5).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places.\n\n$P(X<5)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490218 1072 #uuid "17f155c2-ce06-547c-8fc5-5b0b0c48ed1f" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490218 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.75),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(0.25)^{x-1}(0.75), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.25)^{0}(0.75)+(0.25)^{1}(0.75)+(0.25)^{2}(0.75)+(0.25)^{3}(0.75) \\\\  & ≈0.9961.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490207 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490219 1069 "q-59896" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490219 1071 "![](images/e6/e6c5263a440942da90d5ed6dc6a87e9e6caa0848dfb23c1a904eb828dfcaaeb1.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of rectangles to pentagons is $1:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there is $1$ rectangle for every $\\boxed{}$ pentagons." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490219 1072 #uuid "1816745e-33d2-5605-bad0-d9ea4d6d5feb" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490219 1074 "First, we count the number of rectangles. We see that there are $2$ rectangles.\n\nThen, we count the number of pentagons. We see that there are $2$ pentagons.\n\nSo the ratio of rectangles to pentagons is $2:2.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $1:1$ is equivalent to $2:2.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of rectangles to pentagons is $1:1$ because there is $1$ rectangle for every $1$ pentagon.\"" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490148 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490220 1069 "q-105656" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490220 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490220 1071 "Given that $F(x,y,z)=⟨xy,zy,zx⟩$ and $f(x,y,z)=xyz,$ calculate $∇\\cdot (f\\,F).$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490220 1072 #uuid "19772385-86fe-59c3-9b8a-1626c506e897" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490220 1074 "We will use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot (f\\,F)=f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇\\cdot F$ and $∇f.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨yz,xz,xy⟩, \\\\ ∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(zy)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(zx)=y+z+x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (f\\,F) & =f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f \\\\  & =xyz(x+y+z)+⟨xy,zy,zx⟩\\cdot ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ \\\\  & =xyz(x+y+z)+x{y}^{2}z+xy{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}yz \\\\  & =xyz(x+y+z)+xyz(x+y+z) \\\\  & =2xyz(x+y+z).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490090 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490221 1069 "q-253514" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490221 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490221 1071 "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{5-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have {{field-1}}." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490221 1072 #uuid "2472238f-de89-5651-9808-c29025aa1081" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490221 1074 "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are all the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=5-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/1e/1ea2ed214496ed38ae605e9ac64bebd781a0d5529561fce44d98cca6839d6fbb.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is increasing and converges to $5.$ \n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number in the set $x\\ge 5$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number in the set $x<5$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $5-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}$ is greater than $x$ for any $x<5.$\n\nTherefore, $[5,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[5,\\infty )=5.\n$$\n\nNotice that $5$ is *not* the maximum of $A$ (the set $A$ has no maximum). Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nsup(A)=5\\notin A.\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490197 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490222 1069 "q-79997" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490222 1071 "![](images/ac/acbf4ae592e7912e6e27415ce99f872c4c0ec1737cfa3269635f37f7fa0fe5db.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490222 1072 #uuid "24be045a-a1e0-562b-a001-f17a0f6f12b3" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490222 1074 "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- There is no smallest $y$-value.\n\n- The largest $y$-value is $1.$ This occurs when $x\\to -\\infty .$ However, note that this point is not included in the graph.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-\\infty $ and $1,$ but not including $1.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $-\\infty <y<1,$ which can also be written as  $-\\infty <f(x)<1.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490124 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490223 1069 "q-134280" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490223 1071 "John bought $2$ pounds of chocolate almonds while Rose bought $\\frac{7}{5}$ pound of the same product. How many chocolate almonds did they buy altogether? Express your final answer as a mixed number.\n\nThey bought$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$pounds of chocolate almonds." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490223 1072 #uuid "24d13ec7-deb1-5d3a-8434-3206289f496e" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490223 1074 "To determine the amount of chocolate almonds they bought in total, we have to calculate the following sum:\n\n$$\n2+\\frac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nTo add $2$ and $\\frac{7}{5},$ we need to express the whole number $2$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $5.$\n\nFirst, we write $2$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nThen we put this fraction over a denominator of $5$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\times 5}{1\\times 5}=\\frac{10}{5}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{5}+\\frac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nTo add two fractions with like denominators, we add the numerators and keep the denominators the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{10}{5}+\\frac{7}{5}=\\frac{10+7}{5}=\\frac{17}{5}\n$$\n\nExpressing our answer as a mixed number, we have\n\n$$\n17÷5=3\\,\\text{R}\\,2=3\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, John and Rose bought $3\\frac{2}{5}$ pounds of chocolate almonds." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490142 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490224 1069 "q-69345" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490224 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{5}{8}\\times 3-(8-10)^{-3}.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490224 1072 #uuid "27f65119-9cfd-5858-830b-dc487011110a" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490224 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{8}\\times 3-(8-10)^{-3}=\\frac{5}{8}\\times 3-(-2)^{-3}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that $(-2)^{-3}=\\frac{1}{(-2)^{3}}=-\\frac{1}{8}.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{8}\\times 3-(-2)^{-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{8}\\times 3-(-\\frac{1}{8}) & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{8}\\times \\frac{3}{1}+\\frac{1}{8} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\times 3}{8\\times 1}+\\frac{1}{8} & = \\\\ \\frac{15}{8}+\\frac{1}{8} & = \\\\ \\frac{15+1}{8} & = \\\\ \\frac{16}{8} & = \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490099 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490225 1069 "q-31616" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490225 1071 "What is the value of ${\\int }_{5}^{25}\\frac{3}{x{log}_{5}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490225 1072 #uuid "29e8aaaf-e383-5b29-a6be-9cfefb451c7a" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490225 1074 "Let $u={log}_{5}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $25$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\frac{3}{x{log}_{5}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\frac{1}{{log}_{5}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|){|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490159 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490226 1069 "q-44421" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490226 1071 "A solution to the linear equation $3x-4y=2$ is the ordered pair $(-2,k).$ What is the value of $k?$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490226 1072 #uuid "2b330576-64ef-57a2-934f-fa47fcfddd05" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490226 1074 "Since $(-2,k)$ is a solution to the linear equation $3x-4y=2,$ the equation must turn into a true statement if we substitute $-2$ and $k$ for $x$ and $y.$ So, we replace $x$ with $-2$ and $y$ with $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-4y & =2 \\\\ 3(-2)-4(k) & =2 \\\\ -6-4k & =2 \\\\ -4k & =8 \\\\ k & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490200 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490227 1069 "q-33114" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490227 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490227 1071 "${(2a-{b}^{2})}^{2}=$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490227 1072 #uuid "2be683e9-4308-5a65-9d13-a50367058c8f" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490227 1074 "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(x-y)^{2}={x}^{2}-2xy+{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2a$ and $y={b}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2a-{b}^{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ (2a)^{2}-2(2a)({b}^{2})+{({b}^{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ 4{a}^{2}-4a{b}^{2}+{b}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490089 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490103 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490163 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490228 1069 "q-236388" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490228 1071 "![](images/49/49288e2c082854cfc27a2d48af2d82a96016a3c1394066471d49873e4c75878e.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?\n\nThe range is \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$f(x)<$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490228 1072 #uuid "2c426db9-03a0-51ed-9522-bdc02ff647af" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490228 1074 "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $1.$ This occurs when $x=0.$\n\n- There is no largest $y$-value.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $1$ and $\\infty ,$ including $1.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $1\\le y<\\infty ,$ which can also be written as \n$1\\le f(x)<\\infty .$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490081 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490229 1069 "q-97152" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490229 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490229 1071 "Given that $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.72),$ compute $P(6\\le X\\le 10)$ rounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490229 1072 #uuid "2f7bb618-1c74-5b28-8d56-423cfe55dc4d" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490229 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.72),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(0.28)}^{x-1}(0.72), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(6\\le X\\le 10) & =P(X\\in \\{6,7,8,9,10\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=6)+P(X=7)+P(X=8)+P(X=9)+P(X=10) \\\\  & =f(6)+f(7)+f(8)+f(9)+f(10) \\\\  & ={(0.28)}^{5}(0.72)+{(0.28)}^{6}(0.72)+{(0.28)}^{7}(0.72)+{(0.28)}^{8}(0.72)+{(0.28)}^{9}(0.72) \\\\  & ≈0.0017.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490230 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490198 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490230 1069 "q-96590" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490230 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490230 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.\n\nII. The sum of *any* two $n\\times n$ matrices is always symmetric.\n\nIII. If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490230 1072 #uuid "30380c91-5652-5051-8810-4fc9abb6a29f" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490230 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(AA)^{T} & ={A}^{T}{A}^{T} \\\\  & =AA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their sum is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490231 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490119 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490231 1069 "q-129372" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490231 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490231 1071 "![](images/eb/eb292b77908dbb4590b6bbc49c452215edd8e6668e003e0984074a7ca22bf4ff.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490231 1072 #uuid "30dbc182-568e-5645-9ba2-4447169ef008" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490231 1074 "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $0.$ This occurs when $x=2.$\n\n- There is no largest $y$-value.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $0$ and $\\infty ,$ including $0.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $0\\le y<\\infty ,$ which can also be written as  $0\\le f(x)<\\infty .$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490141 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490232 1069 "q-102142" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490232 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490232 1071 "![](images/7d/7dad50d17444096c60b129131104209b8b7371d546f80d7b0c838baec1df8b4d.png)\n\nWhich of the given points lie on the line $y=-5x+6?$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490232 1072 #uuid "3250f349-fbfe-56f8-96e2-52cb212003b3" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490232 1074 "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(0,5),B(1,2)$ and $C(2,-4).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(0,5)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+6 \\\\ (5) & =-5(0)+6 \\\\ 5 & =6\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does *not* lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(1,2)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+6 \\\\ (2) & =-5(1)+6 \\\\ 2 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(2,-4)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+6 \\\\ (-4) & =-5(2)+6 \\\\ -4 & =-4\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only point $C$ lies on the line." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490112 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490233 1069 "q-169436" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490233 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490233 1071 "Steven took $6$ days to read a book of $45$ pages. If he read the same number of pages each day, then at what rate did Steven read the book?" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490233 1072 #uuid "331d0e38-c03a-5100-b4c4-4b758a51792c" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490233 1074 "The ratio of pages to days is $45:6.$ Expressing this as a fraction, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{45}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can reduce this fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{45÷3}{6÷3}=\\frac{15}{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, Steven read the book at a rate of $\\frac{15}{2}$ pages per day." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490106 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490234 1069 "q-253552" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490234 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490234 1071 "Consider the set $A,$ defined as\n\n$$\nA=\\{(-1)^{n+1}\\cdot n:n\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the supremum of $A?$\n\n$sup(A)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490234 1072 #uuid "332a6eec-904f-5523-bf1a-c62328e3b046" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490234 1074 "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nIf $A$ is a finite set, the supremum of $A$ equals its maximum. \n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{1+1}\\cdot 1 & =1 \\\\ (-1)^{2+1}\\cdot 2 & =-2 \\\\ (-1)^{3+1}\\cdot 3 & =3 \\\\ (-1)^{4+1}\\cdot 4 & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,\\,-2,\\,3,\\,-4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sup(A) & =max\\{1,\\,-2,\\,3,\\,-4\\}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490195 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490235 1069 "q-147042" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490235 1071 "The ratio of T-shirts to pairs of jeans in David's closet is $\\frac{5}{2}.$ If David has $4$ pairs of jeans in his closet, how many T-shirts does David have?" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490235 1072 #uuid "34bd5498-60c5-56d4-9f94-63016d557040" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490235 1074 "The ratio of T-shirts to pairs of jeans in David's closet is $\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of T-shirts and the denominator represents the number of pairs of jeans.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $4$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\times 2}{2\\times 2}=\\frac{10}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, David has $10$ T-shirts in his closet." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490100 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490236 1069 "q-171378" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490236 1071 "$(-1+7.35)^{0}\\times {5}^{-3}=${{field-1}}" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490236 1072 #uuid "355c3927-42d5-52b6-8af5-1388530c1ab2" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490236 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nFirst, we compute the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n(-1+7.35)^{0}\\times {5}^{-3}=(6.35)^{0}\\times {5}^{-3}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that $(6.35)^{0}=1.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6.35)^{0}\\times {5}^{-3} & = \\\\ 1\\times {5}^{-3} & = \\\\ {5}^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{5}^{-3}=\\frac{1}{{5}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{125}.\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490143 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490237 1069 "q-79265" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490237 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490237 1071 "What is the range of the function $f$ defined by the following table?\n\n| $x$ | $-9$ | $-7$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $8$ | $4$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-3$ | $4$ |" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490237 1072 #uuid "3945419b-cbc3-5382-8a24-c717f52753bb" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490237 1074 "The range of $f(x)$ consists of all the outputs that have a corresponding $x$-input.\n\nIn a table, the range consists of all numbers that feature in the row corresponding to $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the range is the set $\\{-3,\\frac{3}{2},4,5,8\\}.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490122 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490238 1069 "q-130033" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490238 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.45),$ compute $P(X=4).$  Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490238 1072 #uuid "3c3e7bcc-b40d-5576-a6ae-56d69405dfc0" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490238 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.45),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(0.55)}^{x-1}(0.45), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4) & =f(4) \\\\  & ={(0.55)}^{3}(0.45) \\\\  & ≈0.0749.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490239 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490184 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490239 1069 "q-102054" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490239 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490239 1071 "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=-2x?$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490239 1072 #uuid "3f93db21-c74e-5e95-a404-6814f4df05d9" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490239 1074 "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x \\\\  & =-2(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,0).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x \\\\  & =-2(1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(1,-2).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-2x.$ \n\n![](images/c6/c632cc2770958b5d73f6e143de0b72d19b2099f9f656ff34f273d88acc4f9cb4.png)" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490087 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490240 1069 "q-130240" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490240 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.9),$ compute $P(X>4).$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490240 1072 #uuid "409b1f60-69c1-5499-92f9-7ef6676bbb2d" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490240 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.9),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(0.1)^{x-1}(0.9), & x=1,2,3… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X>4.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>4) & =1-P(X\\le 4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.1)^{0}(0.9)+(0.1)^{1}(0.9)+(0.1)^{2}(0.9)+(0.1)^{3}(0.9) \\\\  & =0.9999.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>4) & =1-P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =1-0.9999 \\\\  & =0.0001.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490128 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490241 1069 "q-134281" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490241 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490241 1071 "Of the $2$ pounds of chocolate almonds that John bought, he gave his cousin Angela $\\frac{5}{6}$ pounds. As a mixed number, John had{{field-1}}\n$\\,$pounds of chocolate almonds remaining." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490241 1072 #uuid "40b45065-d83b-509a-aac4-0020a7f5f19d" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490241 1074 "To find out how many chocolate almonds John had left, we must calculate the difference $2-\\frac{5}{6}.$\n\nTo subtract $\\frac{5}{6}$ from $2,$ we need to express the whole number $2$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $6.$\n\nFirst, we write $2$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $6$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $6:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\times 6}{1\\times 6}=\\frac{12}{6}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{6}-\\frac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{6}-\\frac{5}{6}=\\frac{12-5}{6}=\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n7÷6=1\\,\\text{R}\\,1=1\\,\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, John has $1\\,\\frac{1}{6}$ pounds of chocolate almonds left." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490088 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490242 1069 "q-31615" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490242 1071 "What is the value of ${\\int }_{4}^{8}\\frac{1}{x{log}_{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490242 1072 #uuid "43a38f2f-26ab-5a55-9c36-de9ec5e565a5" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490242 1074 "Let $u={log}_{2}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{8}\\frac{1}{x{log}_{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{8}\\frac{1}{{log}_{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|){|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490208 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490243 1069 "q-335471" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490243 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{5}{9}),$ compute $P(X<5).$ Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places.\n\n$P(X<5)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490243 1072 #uuid "458360eb-fd03-5244-aa64-17cd1909b422" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490243 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{5}{9}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x-1}(\\frac{5}{9}), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4}{9})}^{0}(\\frac{5}{9})+{(\\frac{4}{9})}^{1}(\\frac{5}{9})+{(\\frac{4}{9})}^{2}(\\frac{5}{9})+{(\\frac{4}{9})}^{3}(\\frac{5}{9}) \\\\  & ≈0.9610.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490101 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490244 1069 "q-102324" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490244 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490244 1071 "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=[\\begin{aligned}-20 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490244 1072 #uuid "45f13db9-2e9d-5ea4-a615-95b4e61aba9f" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490244 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing the components of $x,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}-20 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]=-12+8=-4 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-20 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]=16+6=22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 22\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490098 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490245 1069 "q-97139" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490245 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490245 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.3),$ compute $P(X\\le 4).$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490245 1072 #uuid "4c144938-ebf0-5b6b-9305-5aba70df7465" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490245 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.3),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(0.7)}^{x-1}(0.3), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & ={(0.7)}^{0}(0.3)+{(0.7)}^{1}(0.3)+{(0.7)}^{2}(0.3)+{(0.7)}^{3}(0.3) \\\\  & =0.7599.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490203 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490246 1069 "q-147047" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490246 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490246 1071 "Sophie is baking a pie. The recipe for the pie dough states that the ratio of sugar to melted butter is $\\frac{3}{2}.$ If she decides to use $4$ cups of melted butter, how many cups of sugar does she need to use?" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490246 1072 #uuid "4cce5310-2253-544b-a45b-5b307f4192ed" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490246 1074 "We are given that the ratio of sugar to melted butter is $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of cups of sugar and the denominator represents the number of cups of melted butter.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $4$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 2}{2\\times 2}=\\frac{6}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $4$ cups of melted butter, Sophie must use $6$ cups of sugar." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490150 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490247 1069 "q-81108" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490247 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490247 1071 "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $2{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{41}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{41}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{41}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{41}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{41} \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{41}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490247 1072 #uuid "4d4f28f0-6e57-5f1c-b187-80e59b881c47" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490247 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =x\\cdot {q}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{41} \\\\ -9 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{41}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{41}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{41}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-15+0-8 \\\\  & =-23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n2{x}_{2}=2\\cdot (-23)=-46.\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490091 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490248 1069 "q-34817" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490248 1071 "What is the range of the function $f$ defined by the following table?\n\n| $x$ | $-4.5$ | $-3$ | $-2.5$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $29.25$ | $15$ | $11.25$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $5.25$ |" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490248 1072 #uuid "4f0badb4-976a-5d37-8143-b285dc344b75" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490248 1074 "The range of $f(x)$ consists of all the outputs that have a corresponding $x$-input.\n\nIn a table, the range consists of all numbers that feature in the row corresponding to $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the range is the set $\\{29.25,15,11.25,3,0,-1,5.25\\}.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490175 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490249 1069 "q-331503" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490249 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490249 1071 "A fair coin is flipped $5$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that the coin lands on heads at most $3$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490249 1072 #uuid "510156d7-b5c0-5e8b-8cd5-32b7f7e0537d" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490249 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the coin lands on heads.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ flips.\n\n- The probability of landing heads is $p=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})}^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at most $3$ of the $5$ flips are heads. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{0}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4} \\\\  & \\,+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =(1+5+10+10)\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5} \\\\  & =26\\cdot \\frac{1}{32} \\\\  & =0.813,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490250 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490154 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490250 1069 "q-22437" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490250 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490250 1071 "Which of the following shows a plot of the line $y=-3?$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490250 1072 #uuid "52e56069-89cb-5660-84b2-9dbd8085ccea" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490250 1074 "On the line $y=-3,$ every point has a $y$-coordinate of $-3.$\n\nSo, the plot of $y=-3$ is the following horizontal line:\n\n![](images/92/92fb438bbb86c3acd391be0076c1c7b81c4f3e1d076b4ed4b05874fd7649a3b6.png)" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490161 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490172 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490251 1069 "q-237305" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490251 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490251 1071 "The equation of the vertical line in the $xy$-plane that passes through the point $(7,4)$ is\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490251 1072 #uuid "5491ad4f-b530-5712-8610-611282a460f1" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490251 1074 "Since the vertical line should pass through $(7,4)$, the $x$-value for every point on the line is $7.$ Hence, the equation of that line is \n\n$$\nx=7.\n$$\n\n![](images/0f/0f3e14c92e8e31beb12a2e33aa06fa1b9d349fab4688c39a45d4039859f18271.png)" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490102 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490252 1069 "q-60454" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490252 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490252 1071 "Sophia bought $14$ peaches for $$4.$ What is the price per peach?" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490252 1072 #uuid "585e1f16-1ef5-574f-9665-67875bdab00b" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490252 1074 "The ratio of dollars to peaches is $4:14.$ Expressing this as a fraction, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{14}\n$$\n\nWe can reduce this fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{4÷2}{14÷2}=\\frac{2}{7}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the price of each peach is $\\frac{2}{7}$ of one dollar." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490111 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490253 1069 "q-102150" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490253 1071 "Which of the following shows a plot of the line $x=1?$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490253 1072 #uuid "58f5d0a1-acf5-5310-991a-59bdebc0ea64" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490253 1074 "On the line $x=1,$ every point has an $x$-coordinate of $1.$\n\nSo, the plot of $x=1$ is the following vertical line:\n\n![](images/c5/c5eae02d54bf108bd88a4677dcaf52ea0fe8a0f61c77439886dd6635ab7e744d.png)" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490108 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490254 1069 "q-131289" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490254 1071 "Given that $F(x,y,z)=⟨{y}^{2}z,{x}^{2}+z,{x}^{2}⟩$ and $f(x,y,z)=x+y-z,$ calculate $∇\\cdot (f\\,F).$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490254 1072 #uuid "5cb7d692-f924-57ab-bd09-dd8cc202af02" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490254 1074 "We will use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\cdot (f\\,F)=f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇\\cdot F$ and $∇f.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨1,1,-1⟩, \\\\ ∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2})=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (f\\,F) & =f\\,∇\\cdot F+F\\cdot ∇f \\\\  & =(x+y-z)\\cdot 0+⟨{y}^{2}z,{x}^{2}+z,{x}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =0+{y}^{2}z+{x}^{2}+z-{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}^{2}z+z \\\\  & =z({y}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490255 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490115 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490255 1069 "q-97154" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490255 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490255 1071 "Given that $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{1}{7}),$ compute $P(5\\le X<8)$ rounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490255 1072 #uuid "5e3a6ede-352d-5bbc-898c-f52f077d9252" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490255 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{1}{7}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{6}{7})}^{x-1}(\\frac{1}{7}), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5\\le X<8) & =P(X\\in \\{5,6,7\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=5)+P(X=6)+P(X=7) \\\\  & =f(5)+f(6)+f(7) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{6}{7})}^{4}(\\frac{1}{7})+{(\\frac{6}{7})}^{5}(\\frac{1}{7})+{(\\frac{6}{7})}^{6}(\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\  & ≈0.1999.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490256 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490104 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490256 1069 "q-175956" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490256 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490256 1071 "${2}^{-3}\\times (\\frac{8}{5}+4)=${{field-1}}" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490256 1072 #uuid "5fe451b2-edf1-59c7-ba6b-aec83b3dbee5" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490256 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that ${2}^{-3}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}}.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-3}\\times (\\frac{8}{5}+4) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{{2}^{3}}\\times (\\frac{8}{5}+4) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}\\times (\\frac{8}{5}+4) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}\\times \\frac{8+20}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}\\times \\frac{28}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\times 28}{8\\times 5} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\times 4\\times 7}{2\\times 4\\times 5} & = \\\\ \\frac{1\\times \\cancel{4}\\times 7}{2\\times \\cancel{4}\\times 5} & = \\\\ \\frac{7}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490257 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490116 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490257 1069 "q-71695" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490257 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490257 1071 "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=2x+2.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490257 1072 #uuid "60940cd3-550d-521c-8bf6-1906baac5be6" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490257 1074 "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=2x+2.$\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=2(0)+2=2,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=2(1)+2=4,$\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=2(2)+2=6,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=2(3)+2=8.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n\nPlotting these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane: \n\n![](images/6b/6b2da4bfc7c768788d143999fb61e76bb3a60facffc35e430d98085b395932f7.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of  $y=2x+2.$\n\n![](images/04/0400a1437f26b74feb16161cf51a88c175ee6837fe1e5d8f8785ba2bb2b9c68b.png)" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490166 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490258 1069 "q-13844" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490258 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490258 1071 "What is the value of ${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{3}{2x{ln}^{2}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490258 1072 #uuid "633f13a2-1c12-5a0e-ade7-b4d04e14c842" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490258 1074 "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x).$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x}\\cdot 4=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡8$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{3}{2x{ln}^{2}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x))^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {(-{u}^{-1})|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{u})|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{2}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{2}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(-\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490085 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490110 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490121 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490171 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490259 1069 "q-253555" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490259 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490259 1071 "Consider the set $A=[1,\\sqrt{5}].$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $sup(A).$\n\nThe supremum of a set $A$ is the {{field-1}} of $A.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490259 1072 #uuid "64fea66a-24ac-5ed4-9315-868a66adf40d" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490259 1074 "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge \\sqrt{5}$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $A.$ Thus, the set $[\\sqrt{5},\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $A.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<\\sqrt{5}$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\sqrt{5},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(A)=min\\,[\\sqrt{5},\\infty )=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sqrt{5}$ is also the maximum of $A.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe supremum of $A$ is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $A.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\ge \\sqrt{5}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $sup(A)=\\sqrt{5}\\in A.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490260 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490144 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490260 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490157 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490260 1069 "q-237286" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490260 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490260 1071 "![](images/b0/b028d2711e3fb28b1d027fc9111b72c9ca5511d12dc33d97305ddd1985fa2c8a.png)\n\nThe equation of the horizontal line shown above is{{field-1}}\n$=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490260 1072 #uuid "6bb3e47d-363c-5767-a7cd-275cd49433de" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490260 1074 "The graph is a horizontal line where the $y$-value of every point is $-3.$ Consequently, its equation is $y=-3.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490126 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490261 1069 "q-131244" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490261 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490261 1071 "Calculate $∇\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}F)$ for the vector field $F$ where\n\n$$\nF={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)\\,i+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)\\,j-x{z}^{4}\\,k.\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490261 1072 #uuid "6d8b3a40-13c2-5419-a866-6118c0d389c8" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490261 1074 "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $∇\\cdot (kF)=k∇\\cdot F$\n\n- $∇\\cdot (F+G)=∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot G.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}F)=\\frac{3}{2}∇\\cdot F.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\cdot F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y))+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-x{z}^{4}) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)-4x{z}^{3} \\\\  & =4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)-4x{z}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}F) & =\\frac{3}{2}∇\\cdot F \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)-4x{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)-6x{z}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490177 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490262 1069 "q-44433" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490262 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490262 1071 "Which of the following ordered pairs is a solution to $y=-\\frac{x}{6}?$\n\nI. $(-18,-3)$\n\nII. $(6,1)$\n\nIII. $(12,-2)$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490262 1072 #uuid "6e011359-1ec4-59a5-9ee5-39dc3634d579" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490262 1074 "To check whether each point is a solution to the equation, we substitute it into the equation and see if it results in a true statement.\n\nFirst, we substitute $(-18,-3)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ (-3) & =-\\frac{(-18)}{6} \\\\ -3 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a false statement, so $(-18,-3)$ is *not* a solution to the equation.\n\nNext, we substitute $(6,1)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ (1) & =-\\frac{6}{6} \\\\ 1 & =-1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is again a false statement, so $(6,1)$ is also *not* a solution to the equation.\n\nFinally, we substitute $(12,-2)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{x}{6} \\\\ (-2) & =-\\frac{12}{6} \\\\ -2 & =-2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a true statement, so $(12,-2)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\nSo, out of the three ordered pairs given, the only pair that is a solution to the equation is III $(12,-2).$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490109 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490263 1069 "q-60438" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490263 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490263 1071 "Elena writes $150$ words in $3$ minutes. If she writes at a steady pace, then what statement describes the unit rate?" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490263 1072 #uuid "6f0e951c-f617-594c-9180-7fe86b2cb1e4" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490263 1074 "The ratio of written words to minutes is $150:3.$ So we start with the ratio $150:3$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{150}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{150÷3}{3÷3}=\\frac{50}{1}\n$$\n\nConsequently, Elena writes $50$ words per minute." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490075 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490264 1069 "q-143682" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490264 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490264 1071 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}4 & \\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490264 1072 #uuid "7243696d-a88c-5019-9844-41fc2be79db7" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490264 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}3 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}4 & \\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\"" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490265 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490123 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490265 1069 "q-44453" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490265 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490265 1071 "A horizontal line and a vertical line meet at the point $(1,4).$ What are the equations of the vertical and horizontal lines, respectively?" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490265 1072 #uuid "72f1e9f4-dfe2-5157-9a7e-252993718bb0" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490265 1074 "Since the vertical line should pass through $(1,4)$, the $x$-value for every point on the line is $1.$ Hence, the equation of that line is $x=1.$\n\nSince the horizontal line should pass through $(1,4)$, the $y$-value for every point on the line is $4.$ Hence, the equation of that line is $y=4.$\n\n![](images/16/16e33da178489ccfc16b3e4cef5632d95df18b50ece5ae859f666120827da929.png)" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490153 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490266 1069 "q-70975" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490266 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490266 1071 "Find a normal vector to the plane\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,8,-1⟩+t⟨-1,1,7⟩+s⟨3,-5,1⟩,\\,t,\\,s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490266 1072 #uuid "7340caf5-7f57-5ed9-b918-72cd124f498f" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490266 1074 "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨-1,1,7⟩$ and $w=⟨3,-5,1⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =36i+22j+2k \\\\  & =⟨36,22,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨36,22,2⟩.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490156 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490267 1069 "q-68375" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490267 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490267 1071 "${({3}^{-1})}^{-4}=$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490267 1072 #uuid "79c1f428-3f0c-56d7-8ace-b8cfe3cf2061" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490267 1074 "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents and evaluate:\n\n$$\n{({3}^{-1})}^{-4}={3}^{(-1)\\cdot (-4)}={3}^{4}=81\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490176 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490268 1069 "q-134277" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490268 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490268 1071 "Peter, Steve, Chelsea, and Laura each bought $3$ ounces of gummies and $4$ ounces of chocolates. In total, they bought$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ounces of candy." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490268 1072 #uuid "7a9fb5ec-479f-5a41-8212-79254e42e93a" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490268 1074 "We must write and evaluate an expression representing the total amount of candy.\n\nSince each of them bought $3$ ounces of gummies and $4$ ounces of chocolates, the amount of candy each person bought, in ounces, is:\n\n$$\n{{3}_{⏟}}_{\\text{gummies}}+{{4}_{⏟}}_{\\text{chocolates}}\n$$\n\nSince *each* person purchased this amount of candy, and there are $4$ people, we must multiply the amount of candy by $4.$ Therefore, the total amount of candy is:\n\n$$\n{{(3+4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{each person}}\\times {{4}_{⏟}}_{\\text{people}}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the expression, we follow the order of operations with parentheses.\n\nFirst, we perform the operations inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3+4)\\times 4 & =(3+4)\\times 4 \\\\  & =7\\times 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply:\n\n$$\n7\\times 4=28\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490269 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490180 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490269 1069 "q-134283" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490269 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490269 1071 "Rose decides to eat $\\frac{2}{5}$ of her chocolate almonds. How many pounds of almonds does she decide to eat?\n\n*Remember that Rose has $\\frac{7}{5}$ of a pound of chocolate almonds.*\n\nRose decides to eat\n{{field-1}}\npounds of chocolate almonds." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490269 1072 #uuid "7b9cad24-adba-5e25-92cf-4f2a7c2a1894" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490269 1074 "We know that Rose has $\\frac{7}{5}$ of a pound of chocolate almonds.\n\nTo calculate the fraction of a whole pound that Rose decides to eat, we need to multiply $\\frac{2}{5}$ by $\\frac{7}{5}.$\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{5}\\times \\frac{7}{5}=\\frac{2\\times 7}{5\\times 5}=\\frac{14}{25}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Rose decides to eat $\\frac{14}{25}$ pounds of chocolate almonds." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490072 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490129 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490160 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490165 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490183 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490192 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490270 1069 "q-253044" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490270 1071 "Consider the set $A=(-\\infty ,-1).$ Complete the following reasoning to determine $inf(A)$ and $sup(A).$\n\nThe infimum of $A$ is the {{field-1}} of $A,$ and the supremum is the {{field-2}} of $A.$\n\nSince $A$ is {{field-3}}, we have $inf(A)$ {{field-4}}.\n\nSince $A$ is {{field-5}}, we have $sup(A)$ {{field-6}}." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490270 1072 #uuid "7fdea743-d7bb-5faa-abc9-1bdf19e0dbae" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490270 1074 "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A,$ and the supremum is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSince $A$ is unbounded below, $A$ has no lower bounds, and $inf(A)=-\\infty .$\n\nNext, to find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge -1$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $A.$ Thus, the set $[-1,\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $A.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<-1$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<-1.$\n\nTherefore, $[-1,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(A)=min[-1,\\infty )=-1.\n$$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $A$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $A,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $A.$\n\nSince $A$ is $\\text{unbounded below},$ we have $inf(A)=-\\infty .$\n\nSince $A$ is $\\text{bounded above},$ we have $sup(A)=-1.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490162 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490271 1069 "q-134278" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490271 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490271 1071 "There are $14$ boxes of lollipops in stock, each box containing $22$ lollipops. Therefore, there are $\\,${{field-1}}\n$\\,$lollipops in the store." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490271 1072 #uuid "83ea9ab0-b2f6-54ee-a35a-5ac8d6525a5a" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490271 1074 "To calculate the total number of lollipops, we need to multiply $14$ by $22.$\n\nSo, we write it this way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  &  &  & \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}14\\times 2=28\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply the tens:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}14\\times 20=280\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{0}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}28+280=308\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $14\\times 22=308.$\n\nTherefore, there are $308$ lollipops in total." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490272 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490077 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490272 1069 "q-331515" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490272 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490272 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{1}{5}),$ compute the **exact** value of $P(X=4).$\n\n$P(X=4)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490272 1072 #uuid "8633e974-6d10-58b7-b01c-e86dcc646ae6" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490272 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{1}{5}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x-1}(\\frac{1}{5}), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4) & =f(4) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{3}(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{625}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490168 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490273 1069 "q-92871" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490273 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490273 1071 "What is the missing number in the statement below?\n\n$\\,$ $34$ books in $2$ shelves $=\\boxed{}\\,$  books per shelf." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490273 1072 #uuid "87954c9a-1409-5d07-8491-76a8bb88c183" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490273 1074 "The ratio of the number of books to shelves is $34:2.$ So we start with the ratio $34:2$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{34}{2}\n$$\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{34÷2}{2÷2}=\\frac{17}{1}=17\n$$\n\nSo, $34$ books in $2$ shelves $=17$ books per shelf." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490182 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490274 1069 "q-97164" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490274 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490274 1071 "Given that $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{5}{12}),$ compute $P(4<X<7)$ rounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490274 1072 #uuid "89650cb6-ad4a-5818-b55e-8a9dde9d78b4" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490274 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{5}{12}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{7}{12})}^{x-1}(\\frac{5}{12}), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4<X<7) & =P(X\\in \\{5,6\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=5)+P(X=6) \\\\  & =f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{7}{12})}^{4}(\\frac{5}{12})+{(\\frac{7}{12})}^{5}(\\frac{5}{12}) \\\\  & ≈0.0764.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490191 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490275 1069 "q-97142" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490275 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490275 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.95),$ compute $P(X<4).$  Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490275 1072 #uuid "976a3fe7-7c3e-53a0-9a3a-1795aa3e30b7" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490275 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.95),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(0.05)}^{x-1}(0.95), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={(0.05)}^{0}(0.95)+{(0.05)}^{1}(0.95)+{(0.05)}^{2}(0.95) \\\\  & ≈0.9999.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490276 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490201 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490276 1069 "q-141293" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490276 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490276 1071 "${({9}^{-4})}^{3}=$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490276 1072 #uuid "978d0300-c7f9-5a68-9e89-1cdeb54f80bf" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490276 1074 "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{({9}^{-4})}^{3}={9}^{(-4)\\cdot 3}={9}^{-12}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490277 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490145 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490277 1069 "q-44727" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490277 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490277 1071 "$3x({x}^{2}y+2)=$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490277 1072 #uuid "9cfc88f5-5c61-5b90-beb5-64a3476a024f" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490277 1074 "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x({x}^{2}y+2) & = \\\\ 3x({x}^{2}y)+3x(2) & = \\\\ 3\\cdot {x}^{2+1}y+6x & = \\\\ 3{x}^{3}y+6x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490132 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490278 1069 "q-34734" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490278 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490278 1071 "![](images/58/582236fbb64c0ebeda310cb0007435d4044295b232499ff5771ede846a1b6d05.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function shown above?" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490278 1072 #uuid "9d214df3-aafe-5a52-bd67-2c23fbad240f" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490278 1074 "We find the ranges of the individual pieces, and then calculate their union:\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in [-4,-1)$ is $[0,4]$\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in [-1,1)$ is $[-2,2)$\n\nTherefore, the range of $f(x)$ is $[0,4]\\cup [-2,2)=[-2,4].$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490152 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490279 1069 "q-71837" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490279 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490279 1071 "![](images/f8/f886e9f5dfef693d1ea5d5a0a907a886be368549c1b9a4d43dce257d070e2fa2.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y+3x=6$\n\nII. $\\frac{1}{3}y+x=2$\n\nIII. $y=-3x+6$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490279 1072 #uuid "9d3bda62-1eef-5cb9-886b-004877c9a204" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490279 1074 "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,6)$ and $(2,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/d6/d6cb6d026c181d79bda5c809729eb070c554c2025a287577df0330d1dd8a03e7.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y+3x=6.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,6)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6)+3(0) & =6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓ \\\\ (0)+3(2) & =6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y+3x=6$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $\\frac{1}{3}y+x=2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,6)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(6)+(0) & =2 \\\\ 2+0 & =2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,✓ \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(0)+(2) & =2 \\\\ 0+2 & =2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,$\\frac{1}{3}y+x=2$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=-3x+6.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,6)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6) & =-3(0)+6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =-3(2)+6 \\\\ 0 & =-6+6 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-3x+6$ is  an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nAll the given equations represented the line in the graph. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\"" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490280 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490199 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490280 1069 "q-6844" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490280 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490280 1071 "![](images/2c/2c12cd35e66acc00de926a2375460242e46f359548a5112f0d87654485ba9a42.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490280 1072 #uuid "9ea4d21a-4360-5351-8a08-59d8247080d9" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490280 1074 "First, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The lowest $y$-value is $0.$ This occurs at the point $(-1,0).$ However note that this point is not actually included in the graph.\n\n- The highest $y$-value is $4.$ This occurs at the point $(3,4).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $0$ and $4,$ including $4$ but not including $0.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $0<y\\le 4,$ which can also be written as  $0<f(x)\\le 4.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490140 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490281 1069 "q-60443" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490281 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490281 1071 "What is the missing number in the statement below?\n\n$\\,$ $12$ dogs in $6$ houses $=\\boxed{}\\,$  dogs per house." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490281 1072 #uuid "9f8b2137-ad47-5a05-9270-4407d12097e6" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490281 1074 "The ratio of the number of dogs to the number of houses is $12:6.$ So we start with the ratio $12:6$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $6$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12÷6}{6÷6}=\\frac{2}{1}=2\n$$\n\nSo, $12$ dogs in $6$ houses $=2$ dogs per house." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490282 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490181 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490282 1069 "q-111117" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490282 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490282 1071 "$\\frac{x}{2}(6-10{x}^{3})=$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490282 1072 #uuid "a0223a5d-a1d6-5c40-af63-e8dfd73551f8" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490282 1074 "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}(6-10{x}^{3}) & = \\\\ (\\frac{x}{2})(6)+(\\frac{x}{2})(-10{x}^{3}) & = \\\\ (\\frac{1}{2}x)(6)+(\\frac{1}{2}x)(-10{x}^{3}) & = \\\\ (\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6)x+(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-10))(x\\cdot {x}^{3}) & = \\\\ (3)x+(-5)({x}^{1+3}) & = \\\\ 3x-5{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490283 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490074 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490283 1069 "q-60441" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490283 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490283 1071 "Daniel bought $8$ cookies for $$4.$ What statement describes the unit rate?" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490283 1072 #uuid "a2b1554d-f282-5078-95da-a93d4133cf2c" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490283 1074 "The ratio of cookies to dollars is $8:4.$ So we start with the ratio $8:4$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can make a denominator of $1$ by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8÷4}{4÷4}=\\frac{2}{1}\n$$\n\nConsequently, Daniel bought $2$ cookies per dollar." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490284 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490082 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490284 1069 "q-34159" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490284 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490284 1071 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Find $2u+v.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490284 1072 #uuid "a325c3ab-0a0f-53da-bbb5-9670c5e960cd" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490284 1074 "First, we multiply each component of $u$ by $2.$ Then, we add the vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2u+v & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+3 \\\\ 6+1 \\\\ 4+4 \\\\ 8+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490120 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490285 1069 "q-109055" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490285 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490285 1071 "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x?$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490285 1072 #uuid "a5b958d4-e62e-522f-9fd3-554270dc7fdb" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490285 1074 "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{2}(0) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,2).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=8.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{2}(8) \\\\  & =2-4 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(8,-2).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8c562212687c848e2de25b757f59acd70467a085ae2e0713fbeafa355ad5bb5.png)" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490084 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490286 1069 "q-33095" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490286 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490286 1071 "${(\\frac{1}{3}+{x}^{2})}^{2}=$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490286 1072 #uuid "a62d676a-9c38-5dd1-a066-72e04e4e8a97" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490286 1074 "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{2}={a}^{2}+2ab+{b}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $b={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{3}+{x}^{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+2(\\frac{1}{3})({x}^{2})+{({x}^{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{2}+{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490113 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490287 1069 "q-54293" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490287 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490287 1071 "![](images/71/716836dd3dfc93ac26bf9a960d5edcd04da96907b98c39e926a917b7b8feeeee.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nII. $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$\n\nIII. $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490287 1072 #uuid "a635937b-674b-54dd-a770-48fe7b870be8" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490287 1074 "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,2)$ and $(4,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/2b/2b9174d86d123228ae24fffeda4d2e4741e4a776f6671f7eff38340a59ba4d40.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) & =-1(0)+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(4)+2 \\\\ 0 & =-2+2 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,$y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,2)$ and $B(4,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2) & =\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,✓ \\\\ (0) & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)+2 \\\\ 0 & =2+2 \\\\ 0 & =4\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that represented the line in the graph was $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2,$ corresponding to answer choice II. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490079 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490187 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490206 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490288 1069 "q-223782" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490288 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490288 1071 "![](images/0c/0c18987fb16fc67df39e9887f152b2c6f38649b58e4bf469c80feb908eded8d2.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<\\,f(x)$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490288 1072 #uuid "aa05eb09-9ab6-5ac2-b2dd-bab3e1656e0c" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490288 1074 "First, we find the lowest and highest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The lowest $y$-value is $2.$ This occurs at the point $(2,2).$\n\n- The highest $y$-value is $6.$ This occurs at the point $(-2,6).$\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $2$ and $6,$ but not including $2$ nor $6.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $2<y<6,$ which can also be written as $2<f(x)<6.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490204 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490289 1069 "q-110835" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490289 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490289 1071 "$-{a}^{2}(2{a}^{4}-3{a}^{2}-6)=$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490289 1072 #uuid "aad55e06-4983-5ff1-b72a-41466f8cbb81" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490289 1074 "We multiply each term inside the parentheses by the term outside the parentheses, simplify using the rules of exponents, and then we add the results together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{a}^{2}(2{a}^{4}-3{a}^{2}-6) & = \\\\ (-{a}^{2})(2{a}^{4})+(-{a}^{2})(-3{a}^{2})+(-{a}^{2})(-6) & = \\\\ ((-1)\\cdot 2)({a}^{2}\\cdot {a}^{4})+((-1)\\cdot (-3))({a}^{2}\\cdot {a}^{2})+((-1)\\cdot (-6))({a}^{2}) & = \\\\ (-2){a}^{2+4}+(3){a}^{2+2}+(6){a}^{2} & = \\\\ -2{a}^{6}+3{a}^{4}+6{a}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490290 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490127 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490290 1069 "q-160382" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490290 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490290 1071 "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT([\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}])⟶[\\begin{aligned}uv \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{u}{v})\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n  \n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490290 1072 #uuid "abc95782-56f0-5602-a797-77bbd8bc8d07" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490290 1074 "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x=uv$ and $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{u}{v}).$ \n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-\\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}v & u \\\\ \\frac{1}{u} & -\\frac{1}{v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{v})-u\\cdot \\frac{1}{u} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(1,3)}=-2.\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490093 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490291 1069 "q-71829" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490291 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490291 1071 "![](images/c3/c33968d9b5c86e39ba35c88a5f28bb8950178004c763a5c011633b9a060e98a8.png)\n\nIdentify the points that lie on the line $y=4x+2.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490291 1072 #uuid "acf0d12e-cf58-5db7-b66f-2999c47f2d4e" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490291 1074 "The plotted points correspond to the $xy$-pairs $A(-1,-2)$, $B(0,1)$ and $C(1,6).$\n\nTo check whether a point lies on the line, we substitute the coordinates of the point into the equation and determine whether the resulting statement is true.\n\n- Substituting the point $A(-1,-2)$ results in a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (-2) & =4(-1)+2 \\\\ -2 & =-2\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $A$ does lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $B(0,1)$ results in a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (1) & =4(0)+2 \\\\ 1 & =2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $B$ does not lie on the line.\n\n- Substituting the point $C(1,6)$ results in true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x+2 \\\\ (6) & =4(1)+2 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $C$ does lie on the line.\n\nFinally, only points $A$ and $C$ lie on the line." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490094 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490292 1069 "q-169435" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490292 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490292 1071 "Lily bought $8$ small cakes for $$12.$ At what unit price did Lily pay?" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490292 1072 #uuid "b262ffc7-3f09-5b66-b29a-de72ce27ab0c" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490292 1074 "The ratio of dollars to cakes is $12:8.$ Expressing this as a fraction, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{8}\n$$\n\nWe can reduce this fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{12÷4}{8÷4}=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the unit price Lily paid is $\\frac{3}{2}$ of a dollar per cake." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490130 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490293 1069 "q-60053" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490293 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490293 1071 "John bought a bouquet of $4$ roses for $$5.$ What is the price per rose?" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490293 1072 #uuid "b842ef2a-5852-5df6-832b-3f42a5693c4d" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490293 1074 "The ratio of dollars to roses is $5:4.$ Expressing this as a fraction, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can convert this fraction to a decimal by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $25:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\times 25}{4\\times 25}=\\frac{125}{100}=1.25\n$$\n\nConsequently, the cost is $$1.25$ per rose." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490294 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490170 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490294 1069 "q-54965" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490294 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490294 1071 "What is the range of the function $f$ defined by the following table?\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490294 1072 #uuid "b868c541-58e5-5131-8ec5-c685d5ff37ca" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490294 1074 "The range of $f(x)$ consists of all the outputs that have a corresponding $x$-input.\n\nIn a table, the range consists of all numbers that feature in the row corresponding to $f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the range is the set $\\{1,2,3,4\\}.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490073 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490295 1069 "q-12613" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490295 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490295 1071 "![](images/b0/b051af888048466c4cf4533b3c4f289f7715ee3d5ce0f507d33f49389012392a.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the horizontal line shown above?" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490295 1072 #uuid "b8ace067-214c-5217-8009-450b47fbf196" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490295 1074 "The graph is a horizontal line where the $y$-value of every point is $6.$ Consequently, its equation is $y=6.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490296 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490083 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490296 1069 "q-60149" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490296 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490296 1071 "There are $60$ peaches in Sophia's store. The ratio of peaches to pears is $6:5.$ How many pears are there in Sophia's store?" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490296 1072 #uuid "bad4ad2c-65e9-50c0-a0af-a1c173627efa" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490296 1074 "We start with the ratio $6:5$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of peaches and the denominator represents the number of pears.\n\nWe can make a numerator of $60$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6\\times 10}{5\\times 10}=\\frac{60}{50}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $50$ pears in Sophia's store." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490297 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490096 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490297 1069 "q-92896" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490297 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490297 1071 "John bought $25$ candies for $$3.$ What is the price per candy?" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490297 1072 #uuid "bc23fb40-881c-569e-9c33-6084a7f24a37" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490297 1074 "The ratio of dollars to candies is $3:25.$ Expressing this as a fraction, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{25}\n$$\n\nWe can convert this fraction to a decimal by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 4}{25\\times 4}=\\frac{12}{100}=0.12\n$$\n\nConsequently, the cost is $$0.12$ per candy." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490298 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490092 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490298 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490138 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490298 1069 "q-134282" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490298 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490298 1071 "After Angela takes her share of John's chocolate almonds, the weight of John's remaining chocolate almonds is$\\,${{field-1}}\n$\\,$than the weight of Rose's chocolate almonds because $1\\frac{1}{6}$\n{{field-2}} $\\frac{7}{5}.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490298 1072 #uuid "bc49bd25-7e09-53de-af63-f601d564b8e6" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490298 1074 "John has $1\\frac{1}{6}$ pounds of chocolate almonds remaining, and Rose has $\\frac{7}{5}$ pounds of this product.\n\nWe cannot compare the fractions directly as they don't have a common denominator.\n\nWe can make a common denominator of $6\\times 5=30.$\n\nTo put $1\\frac{1}{6}$ over a denominator of $30,$ we first convert to an improper fraction and then multiply the numerator and denominator by $5:$\n\n$$\n1\\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{7}{6}=\\frac{7\\times 5}{6\\times 5}=\\frac{35}{30}\n$$\n\nTo put $\\frac{7}{5}$ over a denominator of $30,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $6:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{5}=\\frac{7\\times 6}{5\\times 6}=\\frac{42}{30}\n$$\n\nWe can now compare the fractions $\\frac{35}{30}$ and $\\frac{42}{30}$ by looking at the numerators only.\n\nSince $35<42,$ the correct statement must be:\n\n$$\n\\frac{35}{30}<\\frac{42}{30}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is\n\n> \"After Angela takes her part of John's chocolate almonds, the weight of John's remaining chocolate almonds is $<$ than the weight of Rose's chocolate almonds because $1\\frac{1}{6}<\\frac{7}{5}.$\"" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490299 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490097 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490299 1069 "q-60307" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490299 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490299 1071 "To prepare a homemade nasal spray, the ratio of salt (in grams) to cups of water is $\\frac{5}{2}.$ If Eliza wants to make a nasal spray with $15$ grams of salt, how many cups of water should she use?" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490299 1072 #uuid "bdac105a-b535-5e32-bc94-795efb94b947" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490299 1074 "We are given that the ratio of salt (in grams) to cups of water is $\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the amount of salt and the denominator represents the number of cups of water.\n\nWe can make a numerator of $15$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\times 3}{2\\times 3}=\\frac{15}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $15$ grams of salt, Eliza must use $6$ cups of water." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490117 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490300 1069 "q-276116" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490300 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490300 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.8),$ compute the **exact** value of $P(X>3).$\n\n$P(X>3)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490300 1072 #uuid "c2bbdab6-51f8-5199-a746-5b96164dbacd" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490300 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.9),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(0.2)^{x-1}(0.8), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X>3.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\nP(X>3)=1-P(X\\le 3)\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.2)^{0}(0.8)+(0.2)^{1}(0.8)+(0.2)^{2}(0.8) \\\\  & =0.992.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & =1-0.992 \\\\  & =0.008.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490095 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490301 1069 "q-109037" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490301 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490301 1071 "A solution to the linear equation $y=4x-11$ is the ordered pair $(2,h).$ What is the value of $h?$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490301 1072 #uuid "c3d050d6-291f-571b-896c-061ed6a76fc9" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490301 1074 "Since $(2,h)$ is a solution to the linear equation $y=4x-11,$ the equation must turn into a true statement if we substitute $2$ and $h$ for $x$ and $y.$ So, we replace $x$ with $2$ and $y$ with $h,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4x-11 \\\\ (h) & =4(2)-11 \\\\ h & =8-11 \\\\ h & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490105 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490302 1069 "q-335472" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490302 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490302 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{3}{4}),$ compute the **exact** value of $P(X<5).$\n\n$P(X<5)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490302 1072 #uuid "c5de4d6c-cfeb-57f1-bb0f-e9ef45447169" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490302 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{3}{4}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x-1}(\\frac{3}{4}), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{0}(\\frac{3}{4})+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{1}(\\frac{3}{4})+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}(\\frac{3}{4})+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{255}{256}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490178 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490303 1069 "q-31551" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490303 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490303 1071 "${(3+2{w}^{3})}^{2}=$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490303 1072 #uuid "c9bc34bd-c944-573e-9a8a-bd4bb9a0f491" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490303 1074 "We will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{2}={a}^{2}+2ab+{b}^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=3$ and $b=2{w}^{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3+2{w}^{3})^{2} & = \\\\ {3}^{2}+2(3)(2{w}^{3})+(2{w}^{3})^{2} & = \\\\ 9+12{w}^{3}+4{w}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490167 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490304 1069 "q-162510" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490304 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490304 1071 "Construct the vector field that has the stream function $ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{1-y}.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490304 1072 #uuid "cae20d6b-2ae5-5a13-a8c2-7e9cf70b768d" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490304 1074 "A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $ψ(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{e}^{1-y}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{1-y}, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{1-y}) \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{1-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{1-y}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & =P\\,i+Q\\,j \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{1-y}\\,i-2x{e}^{1-y}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490305 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490188 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490305 1069 "q-100388" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490305 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490305 1071 "${({(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})}^{-4}=$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490305 1072 #uuid "d02b196c-7964-5a35-ad18-39ecb861ac94" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490305 1074 "To raise a power to another power, we simply multiply the exponents:\n\n$$\n{({(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})}^{-4}={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2\\cdot (-4)}={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-8}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate ${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-8},$ we find the reciprocal of the base $\\frac{1}{2}$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-8}={2}^{8}=256\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490078 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490164 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490306 1069 "q-237295" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490306 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490306 1071 "![](images/b1/b185732774d819b89f8da5c4862ea86d9bd4d2643629c3f4156d96634eeccbc4.png)\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the vertical line shown above is{{field-1}}\n$=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490306 1072 #uuid "d1122b7b-df38-56a5-b524-7f63741706f2" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490306 1074 "The graph is a vertical line where the $x$-value of every point is $7.$ Consequently, its equation is $x=7.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490080 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490209 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490307 1069 "q-180732" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490307 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490307 1071 "![](images/18/18db54f9403debe31356649f06a92622142dbe90aaa9236489cea1e312f555bc.png)\n\nThe equation of the horizontal line shown above is{{field-1}}\n$=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490307 1072 #uuid "d16b32fa-a461-50f2-a556-45f9c139382e" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490307 1074 "The graph is a horizontal line where the $y$-value of every point is $2.$ Consequently, its equation is $y=2.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490107 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490137 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490308 1069 "q-237297" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490308 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490308 1071 "![](images/86/86f10c07e576676ddc2e7a51e6132f5978feb7dff3173c097e8286fc1d8641bf.png)\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the line shown above is{{field-1}}\n$=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490308 1072 #uuid "d4510c05-0038-50e3-b00f-e4a77a1bb718" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490308 1074 "The graph is a vertical line where the $x$-value at every point equals $3.$ Consequently, its equation is $x=3.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490135 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490309 1069 "q-93990" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490309 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490309 1071 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490309 1072 #uuid "d897469f-7656-5297-824e-0adce50d0f86" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490309 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P,$ $Q,$ and $R.$\"" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490310 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490086 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490310 1069 "q-332366" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490310 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490310 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{1}{2}),$ compute $P(X=6).$  Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places.\n\n$P(X=6)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490310 1072 #uuid "da4631ee-615f-5d28-ba69-71a6d6cc3214" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490310 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{1}{2}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x-1}(\\frac{1}{2}), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=6) & =f(6) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & ≈0.0156.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490186 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490311 1069 "q-130032" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490311 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490311 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.7),$ compute $P(X=4).$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490311 1072 #uuid "ddebff9a-c321-5053-81a7-09bc2befcfb5" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490311 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.7),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(0.3)}^{x-1}(0.7), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4) & =f(4) \\\\  & ={(0.3)}^{3}(0.7) \\\\  & =0.0189.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490169 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490312 1069 "q-59828" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490312 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490312 1071 "If $10$ children ate $15$ cakes, what number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of eaten cakes to children is $3:\\boxed{}\\,$ because there are $3$ eaten cakes for every $\\boxed{}\\,$ children." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490312 1072 #uuid "ddf5db22-7c12-5596-b4f6-5a61a8187c11" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490312 1074 "Since $10$ children can eat $15$ cakes, the ratio of cakes to children is $15:10.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,15 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,10 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,5 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,5 &  \\\\ \\,\\,\\,3 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThat means that $3:2$ is equivalent to $15:10.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of eaten cakes to children is $3:2\\,$ because there are $3$ cakes for every $2\\,$ children.\"\n\nThe missing number is $2.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490189 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490313 1069 "q-134279" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490313 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490313 1071 "There are $308$ lollipops at the store. The store manager wants to make packages containing $4$ lollipops each. In total, he can make$\\,${{field-1}}\n$\\,$packages." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490313 1072 #uuid "dfff5d32-78f7-5797-b312-14bdc373c270" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490313 1074 "We know that there are $308$ lollipops in total. Since each package must contain $4$ lollipops,the store manager can make $308÷4$ packages.\n\nWe start by writing down the question using long division notation:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{3\\,\\phantom{|}0\\,\\phantom{|}8}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"250\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr height=\"20\">\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNotice that $3$ (the number of hundreds in $308$) is less than the divisor $4.$ Therefore, we should consider both the hundreds and tens:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$7$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{3\\,\\phantom{|}0\\,\\phantom{|}8}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$↓$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"250\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Divide: $30÷4=7\\,\\text{R}\\,2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Write over tens: $7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"25\">\n<td>Multiply: $4\\times 7=28$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Subtract: $30-28=2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"25\">\n<td>Bring down: $8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNext, we consider the ones:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$7$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#EBF5FB\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{3\\,\\phantom{|}0\\,\\phantom{|}8}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\boxed{\\text{0}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n<td align=\"left\" width=\"250\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Divide: $28÷4=7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Write over ones: $7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"25\">\n<td>Multiply: $4\\times 7=28$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr height=\"20\">\n<td>Subtract: $28-28=0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nWe get the remainder $0$, so the division is done.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n308÷4=77.\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490139 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490314 1069 "q-162612" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490314 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490314 1071 "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nI. $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$\n\nII. $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=x\\,i+2{e}^{y}\\,j$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490314 1072 #uuid "e0611f00-dc8b-5374-b5f7-8b0d695e2534" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490314 1074 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field I doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=1+2{e}^{y}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490149 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490315 1069 "q-93986" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490315 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490315 1071 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 5 & \\sqrt{2} & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490315 1072 #uuid "e09bb1cf-2314-567d-8f07-99c777c75c71" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490315 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{12}\\ne {q}_{21},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$ only.\"" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490316 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490155 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490316 1069 "q-79411" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490316 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490316 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix which has exactly $2$ non-pivot columns?\n\nI. $A$ is  invertible\n\nII. $\\text{rank}(A)=2$\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(A)=2$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490316 1072 #uuid "e3a4804f-9ed9-56b0-80c9-162f94bd57af" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490316 1074 "Since $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=6=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ has $n=6$ columns and $2$ of them are non-pivot ones, it follows that    \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Col}(A))=6-2=4\\ne 6.\n$$\n\n Thus, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=4$,  then $\\text{rank}(A)=4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $n=6$, according to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =6 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =6-4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490196 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490317 1069 "q-334517" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490317 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490317 1071 "Given that $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.25),$ compute $P(3\\le X<7)$ rounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\n$P(3\\le X<7)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490317 1072 #uuid "e519920d-4b78-559e-a669-c1163fd46764" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490317 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.25),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(0.75)^{x-1}(0.25), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\le X<7) & =P(X\\in \\{3,4,5,6\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=3)+P(X=4)+P(X=5)+P(X=6) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4)+f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{2}(0.25)+(0.75)^{3}(0.25)+(0.75)^{4}(0.25)+(0.75)^{5}(0.25) \\\\  & ≈0.3845.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490318 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490205 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490318 1069 "q-162614" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490318 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490318 1071 "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nI. $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-xy\\,j$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=({x}^{2}-2xy)\\,i+({y}^{2}-2xy)\\,j$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)={e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)\\,i-{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3y)\\,j$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490318 1072 #uuid "e9691dde-e78b-59c1-ac5c-550cb0f124bb" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490318 1074 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field I doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x+(-x)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2y-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=(2x-2y)+(2y-2x)=0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does have a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y)+(-3{e}^{3x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3y))=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490319 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490147 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490319 1069 "q-332934" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490319 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490319 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{3}{5}),$ compute the **exact** value of $P(3\\le X\\le 6).$\n\n$P(3\\le X\\le 6)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490319 1072 #uuid "ebe82800-a8b2-58e9-ab4c-047410ac5d4f" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490319 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{3}{5}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{x-1}(\\frac{3}{5}), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\le X\\le 6) & =P(X\\in \\{3,4,5,6\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=3)+P(X=4)+P(X=5)+P(X=6) \\\\  & =f(3)+f(4)+f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}(\\frac{3}{5})+{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}(\\frac{3}{5})+{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{4}(\\frac{3}{5})+{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{5}(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2436}{15625}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490320 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490202 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490320 1069 "q-101444" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490320 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490320 1071 "![](images/ed/eda2132e8e050399298b97dc9d36cf27862d6aa4625e7aa870bb4c8fd246e781.png)\n\nWhat ratio describes the number of triangles to the number of pentagons?" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490320 1072 #uuid "ec0c1b9c-5490-5482-a264-c0fe31855e42" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490320 1074 "We count the number of triangles. We see that there are $2$ triangles.\n\nWe count the number of pentagons. We see that there are $4$ pentagons.\n\nSo the ratio of triangles to pentagons is $2:4.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers in a ratio by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,2 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,4 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of triangles to pentagons is $1:2.$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490321 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490173 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490321 1069 "q-49552" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490321 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490321 1071 "What is the value of ${\\int }_{e/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490321 1072 #uuid "ee507cc2-c5c1-5dca-b8f8-28391520da63" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490321 1074 "Let's set $u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4x}\\cdot 4=\\frac{1}{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $e/4$ | ${e}^{3}/4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{e/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\frac{3}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{e/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490322 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490118 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490322 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490179 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490322 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490185 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490322 1069 "q-236465" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490322 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490322 1071 "![](images/fa/fae9ac16ebabea7054903ab75f2305f98c757f8ee7fc4924600774e627ad76b4.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?\n\n$\\,$  The range is $f(x)\\in $\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490322 1072 #uuid "f5d6b2e7-1b54-5bc0-b6ea-58d113518a87" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490322 1074 "We find the ranges of the individual pieces, and then calculate their union:\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in (-3,0]$ is $[-2,0)$\n\n- The range of the branch defined on $x\\in (0,3)$ is $(1,3]$\n\nTherefore, the range of $f(x)$ is $[-2,0)\\cup (1,3].$\n\n**Note:** From the graph, we see that the values $y=0$ and $y=1$ are NOT valid outputs of the function $y=f(x).$ Therefore, we use parentheses $(…)$ instead of brackets $[…]$ at these endpoints." 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490323 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490131 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490323 1069 "q-97131" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490323 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490323 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{1}{4}),$ compute $P(X=3).$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490323 1072 #uuid "f5dba093-2258-5290-b7aa-4504e51efcbb" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490323 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{1}{4}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{x-1}(\\frac{1}{4}), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490324 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490151 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490324 1069 "q-102354" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490324 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490324 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{1}$, where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490324 1072 #uuid "f89c3382-117b-502e-8af6-ebca79b6f083" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490324 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{1}}{{q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 2+8\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+8+8}{1+4+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{6} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot 3=5.\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490325 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490194 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490325 1069 "q-335524" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490325 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490325 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.4),$ compute the **exact** value of $P(X>4).$\n\n$P(X>4)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490325 1072 #uuid "f9e4e9da-f36b-5b4a-91a7-731a48c88c99" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490325 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.4),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}(0.6)^{x-1}(0.4), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X>4.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\nP(X>4)=1-P(X\\le 4)\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2,3,4\\}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.6)^{0}(0.4)+(0.6)^{1}(0.4)+(0.6)^{2}(0.4)+(0.6)^{3}(0.4) \\\\  & =0.8704.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>4) & =1-P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =1-0.8704 \\\\  & =0.1296.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490326 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490190 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490326 1069 "q-142483" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490326 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490326 1071 "Consider a $8\\times 8$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{rank}(A)=5$ then $\\text{nullity}(A)=$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490326 1072 #uuid "fa527559-21f3-5088-ac1e-a7d1439bef18" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490326 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 5+\\text{nullity}(A) & =8 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =8-5 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490146 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490327 1069 "q-142484" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490327 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490327 1071 "Consider a $8\\times 10$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{rank}(A)=7$ then $\\text{nullity}(A)=$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490327 1072 #uuid "fb06ba3d-1b9f-5681-af01-0922516167aa" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490327 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=10.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 7+\\text{nullity}(A) & =10 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =10-7 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490328 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490136 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490328 1069 "q-81140" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490328 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490328 1071 "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490328 1072 #uuid "fbba5a71-4786-5f34-9118-2344153343aa" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490328 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing the components of $x,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{2}}=3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490329 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490193 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490329 1069 "q-105634" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490329 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490329 1071 "Calculate $∇\\cdot (-3F)$ for the vector field $F$ where\n\n$$\nF=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j-4z\\,k.\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490329 1072 #uuid "fe02f258-a8c1-593e-bacb-46d349ae065b" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490329 1074 "If $F$ and $G$ are vector fields and $k$ is a constant, then we have the following  linearity properties for divergence:\n\n- $∇\\cdot (kF)=k∇\\cdot F$\n\n- $∇\\cdot (F+G)=∇\\cdot F+∇\\cdot G.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (-3F) & =-3∇\\cdot F.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to calculate $∇\\cdot F.$ In doing so, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4z) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4 \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the linearity properties, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot (-3F) & =-3∇\\cdot F \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2) \\\\  & =6(2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490330 1058 #edb/ref 17592186490379 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490330 1062 #uuid "027ab44b-48d6-5961-8e71-f93826f26196" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490330 1063 #edb/ref 17592186490222 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490331 1062 #uuid "032a341e-0565-552a-b03a-ffb8b061f234" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490331 1063 #edb/ref 17592186424327 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490332 1058 #edb/ref 17592186490390 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490332 1062 #uuid "05a87747-f2bd-5213-af84-8786d8887033" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490332 1063 #edb/ref 17592186490284 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490333 1058 #edb/ref 17592186490352 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490333 1062 #uuid "07385d24-63ec-535c-82de-17f626a1096d" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490333 1063 #edb/ref 17592186490258 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490334 1058 #edb/ref 17592186490363 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490334 1062 #uuid "07ae2f09-fe9e-5ba9-a0d7-b2c82c644d07" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490334 1063 #edb/ref 17592186311921 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490335 1062 #uuid "09fbdd8d-ce54-5956-a8aa-a99300b51219" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490335 1063 #edb/ref 17592186490269 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490336 1058 #edb/ref 17592186490359 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490336 1062 #uuid "0bc8636c-0807-5847-a5a8-7748bf0a3cfb" 13194139535279 17592186045417 true] [17592186490336 1063 #edb/re